WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:13.690
- Welcome to our fourth
and final Shulman Lecture

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:14.860
for the semester.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.090
I'm not saying that we
saved the best for last

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.590
but I'm not denying it either.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:23.830
Our speaker today is
Professor Elizabeth Spelke.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:26.720
She is the Marshall Berkman
Professor of Psychology

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.040
at Harvard, where she also co-directs

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.160
the Laboratory for Developmental Studies.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:34.990
She is certainly one of
the world's most prominent

00:00:36.502 --> 00:00:38.520
and impressive cognitive scientists.

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:41.230
And in my only hesitation in saying that

00:00:41.230 --> 00:00:44.380
is that I'm not sure the
word cognitive is necessary.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.690
Liz is also just one of
the world's most prominent

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:49.040
and impressive scientists.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:51.780
This is true in terms
of both concrete methods

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.730
and abstract deep theory.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:57.780
Methodologically, Liz has pioneered

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.220
new ways of reading the minds of babies

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:02.760
with elegant behavioral measures.

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.930
Theoretically, she's used these methods

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:07.880
to transform our
understanding of our own minds

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:09.460
and where they come from.

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:12.740
And she has done this, not just
in one or two case studies,

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:16.320
but for essentially every
primary aspect of our minds

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:18.140
that we care about,

00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:21.930
characterizing what she calls
our core knowledge of objects,

00:01:21.930 --> 00:01:25.230
space, number, physics, geometry,

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.510
social agents, and beyond.

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.690
And I think those categories
are very important

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:32.750
but also sort of a
little dry and abstract.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:34.980
In the context of doing this,

00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.670
Liz has also studied everything

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:39.670
from food and shadows and maps

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:42.600
to race and accents and kinship

00:01:42.600 --> 00:01:44.320
to music and singing,

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:46.580
to a paper in science earlier this year

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.510
that looked at infants appreciation

00:01:48.510 --> 00:01:51.550
of intimacy and saliva sharing.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.650
That's the first time we
have uttered that phrase

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:54.833
in the Shulman seminar.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:02:00.390
In almost any aspect of
these areas and beyond,

00:02:00.390 --> 00:02:01.250
you can find her work

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.500
both at the foundations
and the cutting edge.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:05.720
As one person has called her,

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:07.817
she is the "preeminent researcher

00:02:07.817 --> 00:02:09.507
"of the basic ingredient list

00:02:09.507 --> 00:02:12.917
"from which all human
knowledge is constructed."

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:17.120
And if this is starting to
sound a bit too grandiose,

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:20.610
I will just note that a
profile of her once noted that

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.620
she had summarized on
her lab's bulletin board

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:27.027
her current research program in terms of

00:02:27.027 --> 00:02:31.777
"blocks and holes, talking
blobs and wet animals."

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.850
And I will confess, Liz, I've never known,

00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:36.750
I get the other two,
I've never known quite

00:02:36.750 --> 00:02:39.050
what to make of the wet animals thing.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:41.130
But now that I see the saliva sharing,

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.380
maybe I'm starting to see that.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:45.740
Liz has done all of this

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:47.290
while being one of the most positive

00:02:47.290 --> 00:02:49.140
and nurturing people in our field.

00:02:49.140 --> 00:02:50.870
The list of students she's worked with

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.930
is really a who's who of
cognitive science at this point.

00:02:53.930 --> 00:02:57.350
And she is a role model for so many of us.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:00.443
In fact, if you'll permit one
slightly disturbing story,

00:03:02.738 --> 00:03:05.020
I've only ever had one negative thought

00:03:05.020 --> 00:03:06.880
about Liz in my career.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.100
It was early on and it was my mistake.

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.460
When I was a second year graduate student,

00:03:12.460 --> 00:03:17.460
I think in 1995 or some
pre-historical time like that,

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:20.080
I wrote one of the first
papers I had ever written,

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:23.230
and for some reason it occurred
to me as it happens to one

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:25.660
that like Liz Spelke
needs to read this paper.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:28.370
And so I emailed her out of
the blue as a graduate student

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:30.257
and I said, "Hey, I've done
this work on blah, blah, blah,

00:03:30.257 --> 00:03:31.710
"and would you be willing
to take a look at it?"

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:35.100
And she responded in about like 10 seconds

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.890
by email and said, it was
such a warm and nice message,

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:40.787
saying, "I would love to check that out,

00:03:40.787 --> 00:03:41.620
"please send it to me.

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:43.387
"It sounds so interesting.

00:03:43.387 --> 00:03:45.457
"I'm in France this summer,
please send it along.

00:03:45.457 --> 00:03:46.870
"I'll get you back comments right away."

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:48.380
And I was so excited.

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.693
And she never wrote back to me.

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:55.480
And so I thought that
was sort of the genesis

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:57.060
of this negative thought,

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.670
that for years, I thought
like, wow, she seemed so warm,

00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:02.260
but it was like a con,
she never got back to me.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.690
And then I finally met her years later

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:07.037
and she seemed so nice and I
was hesitant to bring it up.

00:04:07.037 --> 00:04:08.440
And then two years after that,

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:10.960
this is like six years
after I had sent this paper,

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.820
I got the paper back.

00:04:12.820 --> 00:04:14.730
And this is the slightly disturbing story.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:19.590
That paper had been in the
mail on flight TWA 800,

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.620
which, for those of you
who don't remember this,

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:23.260
this is a flight that famously

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:26.840
was on its way to France and
crashed over Long Island Sound.

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:27.990
They recovered the paper

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:29.670
from the bottom of Long Island Sound

00:04:29.670 --> 00:04:31.440
and it had been in the custody

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:34.980
of the agencies investigating
the crash for years.

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:37.830
I don't think they found
any causal connection

00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:38.930
between the work, though.

00:04:38.930 --> 00:04:41.260
So, Liz, I apologize for thinking

00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:43.340
that negative thought about
you for all those years.

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:44.390
It wasn't your fault.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.290
I'll end just by noting that Liz has won,

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:50.400
basically, just all of the honors

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:52.830
that our field has to bestow.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.220
Among many others, she's
a member of the AAAS

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:57.440
and the National Academy of Sciences.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.670
She's a corresponding fellow
of the British Academy

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.170
for the Humanities and Social Sciences.

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:04.830
She and her work have been
profiled in both the New Yorker

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:06.570
and the New York Times.

00:05:06.570 --> 00:05:09.870
She's won the Jean Nicod
Prize, the Kurt Koffka Medal,

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.920
the Heineken Prize for Cognitive Sciences,

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:13.230
the William James Award

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:15.640
from the American Psychological Society,

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:18.060
the Distinguished Scientific
Contribution Award

00:05:18.060 --> 00:05:20.730
from the American
Psychological Association.

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:21.720
I think most important,

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:23.560
she won a Distinguished Alumni Award

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:26.310
from the New Canaan Country
School here in Connecticut.

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:30.140
But she has not yet delivered

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:31.930
a Shulman Lecture here at Yale.

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:34.339
Please welcome Elizabeth Spelke.

00:05:34.339 --> 00:05:37.089
(all applauding)

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:44.300
- I don't know whether to thank you or not

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:45.594
for that introduction.

00:05:45.594 --> 00:05:47.910
(all laughing)

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:48.880
On the one hand,

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:51.670
I'm not gonna talk about
any of those lines of work.

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:53.320
If you wanna know about saliva sharing,

00:05:53.320 --> 00:05:55.250
you're gonna have to ask me over drinks

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:57.203
or something afterwards, okay?

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:01.320
Second, while I might have been innocent

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:03.050
on that particular occasion,

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:06.050
anybody who knows me knows
that I'm way more enthusiastic

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:08.090
about what I say I'm going to read

00:06:08.090 --> 00:06:10.370
than I actually end up
having time to read.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:13.423
So while I might have been
innocent in that particular case,

00:06:14.294 --> 00:06:15.760
you shouldn't conclude across the board

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.043
that that's going to be true.

00:06:19.620 --> 00:06:21.430
One thing I do have to say is,

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:24.990
I'm, again, coming from
Paris and I am so happy

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:26.650
to be here with all of you.

00:06:26.650 --> 00:06:29.140
This is my first real talk,

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:32.760
in-person talk since the pandemic started.

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.170
It's been totally fun.

00:06:35.170 --> 00:06:36.670
I've had a really great day so far

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:38.280
and I'm very happy to be here with you.

00:06:38.280 --> 00:06:39.810
I'm also under strict orders

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:43.240
that I can't go over 45 minutes,

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:45.350
which is one of the reasons
why I'm not gonna talk about

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:48.450
any of the topics that
were on Brian's list.

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:52.010
And also a reason why I've
decided to do something

00:06:52.010 --> 00:06:54.880
that I've actually never
done at a talk before,

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:57.190
which is to start with
the final conclusion

00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:59.550
so that if my optimistic assessment

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:02.450
that I'm gonna be done in 45
minutes turns out to be wrong,

00:07:02.450 --> 00:07:06.330
you won't be missing the
message of this talk.

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:11.330
So there are three general
conclusions to this talk.

00:07:11.610 --> 00:07:16.470
The first is that, between
our perceptual systems

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:21.470
and the systems in which we
use our explicit concepts

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:25.090
and form beliefs and communicate
our beliefs to others,

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:28.520
there's an important
level of representation.

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.630
It's a level of representation

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:34.120
that I think is often not recognized

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:39.040
in either philosophy or the sciences.

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:41.300
I think certainly when I was a student,

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:44.620
I was taught that there
were two kinds of systems.

00:07:44.620 --> 00:07:47.360
In the mind, there were
the automatic systems

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:50.140
that control our
perceptions and our actions,

00:07:50.140 --> 00:07:55.140
and there were the conscious,
deliberate, accessible systems

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:59.000
in which we formulated
and expressed our beliefs,

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:03.910
and that the mind divided
into these two parts.

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:07.130
Today, they're sometimes called
things like thinking fast

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:09.620
versus thinking slow,

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:12.500
peripheral processes
versus central processes,

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:14.650
that this is how the mind divides up.

00:08:14.650 --> 00:08:16.940
But I actually think
there's an important level

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:20.580
of representation
between those two levels.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.640
I don't deny the existence of
either of those two levels,

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:26.877
but I think if we really wanna
understand how our minds work

00:08:26.877 --> 00:08:29.640
and answer some basic questions about

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:32.703
how are we all the same
and how are we different?

00:08:34.233 --> 00:08:35.700
And how do children learn?

00:08:35.700 --> 00:08:40.310
That it really helps to focus
on a level that mediates

00:08:40.310 --> 00:08:44.520
between and remains constant
over all of the changes

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:47.990
in what we perceive and what we believe.

00:08:47.990 --> 00:08:50.870
Now, the second general lesson is that,

00:08:50.870 --> 00:08:55.510
at this level, concepts
that are highly general

00:08:55.510 --> 00:08:57.540
and the capture properties of the world

00:08:57.540 --> 00:08:59.470
that are highly abstract,

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:01.270
actually emerge in development

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:05.000
before concepts that are more specific.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:06.570
And I think this is true,

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:09.410
both when we look over human ontogeny,

00:09:09.410 --> 00:09:11.290
and also when we look over

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:15.650
the evolution of life on earth.

00:09:15.650 --> 00:09:18.380
If that's true, it implies that

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:23.280
the ways in which infants
and other animals are like us

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:27.080
are not that they share our basic reflexes

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:30.983
or sensory experiences, but
that they share our basic,

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:35.720
our sensitivity to the
basic abstract properties

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:40.040
of causality and space and
time that structure our world.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:44.270
Those are the ways in which
other animals are most like us.

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:48.000
And I think this also goes
against widespread intuitions

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:50.690
about what we have in
common with our house cats

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:55.490
and or what we as adults have
in common with newborn infants

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.230
that we might share
some of their emotions,

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:01.850
we might share some of
their sensory experiences,

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:03.410
some of their reflex actions,

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:07.900
but surely not the basic
abstract distinctions

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:09.640
by which we understand the world.

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:12.200
That I think, is how we
intuitively tend to think about

00:10:12.200 --> 00:10:13.680
infants and animals.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:16.920
But I think this research
suggests that that's not right,

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:19.350
and that it's really the abstract concepts

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:22.960
that are most likely to be
common to us at all ages

00:10:22.960 --> 00:10:25.130
and also common to different species,

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:27.470
and hence the first part of my title,

00:10:27.470 --> 00:10:29.560
the primordial blessing of abstraction,

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:32.800
which I'll try to unpack
over the course of this talk.

00:10:32.800 --> 00:10:35.080
And then the third lesson

00:10:36.870 --> 00:10:39.140
goes against sort of the
most fundamental thing

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:42.850
that people were always saying
to me when I was a student,

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:43.683
and then again,

00:10:43.683 --> 00:10:45.420
when I started doing
research in this field,

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:47.930
and would find evidence
that some abilities

00:10:47.930 --> 00:10:49.523
seem to be innate.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:53.800
The idea was that
something is either innate

00:10:53.800 --> 00:10:55.453
or it is learned.

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.637
And if it's innate, then it's not learned,

00:10:58.637 --> 00:11:01.980
and if it's learned, then it's not innate.

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:05.150
And that's all of course, true.

00:11:05.150 --> 00:11:09.040
But what people seem to often
think followed from that

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:12.110
was that the more innate
knowledge you have,

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:13.670
the less you have to learn,

00:11:13.670 --> 00:11:15.120
because all the stuff that you know,

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:17.350
that's innate is not learned.

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:20.550
I actually think that the reverse is true,

00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:24.730
that the more innate concept,
well, let me put it this way.

00:11:24.730 --> 00:11:28.563
If we lived in a perfect unchanging world,

00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:32.310
where throughout the
evolution of life on earth,

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:34.530
the environment stayed exactly the same

00:11:34.530 --> 00:11:37.170
and predictable in exactly the same ways,

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:38.790
then maybe it would be true that

00:11:38.790 --> 00:11:41.110
the more innate concepts you had,

00:11:41.110 --> 00:11:42.780
the less you needed to learn.

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:45.320
But in the actual world
that all animals live in,

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:47.760
the world is changing and it's uncertain.

00:11:47.760 --> 00:11:52.030
And in that circumstance, the
more innate concepts you have,

00:11:52.030 --> 00:11:53.890
the more of a learning problem you face

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:57.170
because you have to discover
which concepts to call on

00:11:57.170 --> 00:11:58.700
in any given situation,

00:11:58.700 --> 00:11:59.767
which ones are gonna be useful

00:11:59.767 --> 00:12:01.640
and what they're going to be useful for.

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:06.020
And this is what I call the
curse of a compositional mind.

00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:10.030
So what I wanna do today
is, my talk is in two parts.

00:12:10.030 --> 00:12:12.580
The first part is going to be an attempt

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:15.150
to convey to you in 30 minutes,

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:19.130
what is soon to appear in
a humongously long book,

00:12:19.130 --> 00:12:21.970
which covers exactly
one year in human life.

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:25.130
It starts at birth and it
goes to one year of age,

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:27.150
and tries to talk about what I think

00:12:28.280 --> 00:12:30.880
the cognitive sciences have learned about

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:34.090
what infants know over the
course of that first year,

00:12:34.090 --> 00:12:36.730
especially in the early
part of that first year.

00:12:36.730 --> 00:12:40.440
So I'm gonna spend two thirds
of my talk focused on that.

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:42.010
And then I wanna turn to the problem

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:43.110
that I'm working on now,

00:12:43.110 --> 00:12:45.740
in which I hope will
someday become volume two

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:50.740
of the next book and the next
and last book in this series.

00:12:51.450 --> 00:12:53.810
I wanna turn to the question
of how children learn

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:57.220
and in particular, focus on what I think

00:12:57.220 --> 00:13:01.030
is a learning process
that's unique to our species

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:04.600
and that helps account for
how we could be so similar

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:07.830
to other animals at birth in our abstract,

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:10.170
the abstract concepts that
we apply to the world,

00:13:10.170 --> 00:13:11.600
and yet find ourselves

00:13:11.600 --> 00:13:13.530
on such a different path of development

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:16.820
when we start to grow explicit knowledge.

00:13:16.820 --> 00:13:19.590
So to start with what infants know,

00:13:19.590 --> 00:13:21.890
let me see if I can get
all this on one slide.

00:13:21.890 --> 00:13:23.840
They know that there are objects,

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:25.510
that the world that they have entered

00:13:25.510 --> 00:13:27.890
is going to have bodies
in it that are solid,

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:30.140
then that move continuously through space

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:33.563
and that interact with
one another on contact.

00:13:34.850 --> 00:13:36.680
And I think they may also,

00:13:36.680 --> 00:13:39.690
this knowledge may be
accompanied by capacities

00:13:39.690 --> 00:13:42.360
to form mental simulations
of these objects

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:44.810
and their interactions.

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:48.040
They know that there are places
and that places are arrayed

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:51.450
on a surface over which
you can navigate that

00:13:51.450 --> 00:13:53.900
that surface, depending
on how it's contoured,

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:57.263
will afford navigation on
some paths and not others.

00:13:58.120 --> 00:14:01.410
And with that surface,
as you travel over it,

00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:03.320
you can learn how,

00:14:03.320 --> 00:14:05.580
what different paths
connect different places

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:07.623
in the space to one another.

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:11.660
Most tentatively, I
think that they know that

00:14:11.660 --> 00:14:13.780
there are living beings in the world

00:14:13.780 --> 00:14:17.830
with characteristic forms
and characteristic functions

00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:20.270
for us and for themselves

00:14:20.270 --> 00:14:22.940
that live anchored to this surface,

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:26.680
and their forms and functions
both may be captured initially

00:14:26.680 --> 00:14:29.260
by something like shape skeletons.

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:31.020
And now coming away from the tentative

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:33.380
more to things where
there's more evidence.

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:35.320
I think that they know there are agents

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:37.410
that cause their own motion

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:39.980
and by moving cause changes in the world,

00:14:39.980 --> 00:14:44.980
and that they move by
pursuing goals efficiently

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:50.030
to bring them valued
and accessible objects.

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:51.880
I think they know there are social beings

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:53.690
who share their experience,

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:55.790
who experience the world as we do,

00:14:55.790 --> 00:14:58.240
and who share their
experiences with one another

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:00.513
or with us in states of engagement.

00:15:01.724 --> 00:15:04.360
And I think they know
that they have a system

00:15:04.360 --> 00:15:09.010
for representing when any of
the entities I just described

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:13.100
congregate in groups or are
arrayed in sequences of events.

00:15:13.100 --> 00:15:14.910
They have a system for representing

00:15:14.910 --> 00:15:16.990
their approximate numerosity,

00:15:16.990 --> 00:15:20.120
a system that they can
use for diverse purposes,

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:22.490
but maybe especially for learning,

00:15:22.490 --> 00:15:25.760
probabilistic learning about
the properties of the world

00:15:25.760 --> 00:15:28.700
and also making decisions
and planning actions.

00:15:28.700 --> 00:15:31.630
So these different
things that infants know,

00:15:31.630 --> 00:15:36.040
I think stem from six
different cognitive systems

00:15:36.040 --> 00:15:38.190
that I call systems of core knowledge

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:42.480
that have a number of
interesting properties in common.

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:46.440
The most important is that they
center on abstract concepts

00:15:46.440 --> 00:15:48.520
that are interconnected to one another.

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:53.080
And here, I've just put pointers
to the distinctive concepts

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:55.550
that I think arise

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:58.723
from each of these different core systems.

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:01.770
But for the rest of the properties,

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:03.280
I wanna take the place system

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:06.490
to quickly kind of unpack them for you.

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:08.620
The second property of these core systems

00:16:08.620 --> 00:16:10.150
is that they're limited.

00:16:10.150 --> 00:16:14.540
So when infants or inexperienced animals

00:16:14.540 --> 00:16:17.310
begin to navigate through space,

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:19.610
they're navigating through
a very rich environment

00:16:19.610 --> 00:16:21.880
where they have multiple
systems that they can use

00:16:21.880 --> 00:16:23.940
to pick out landmark objects,

00:16:23.940 --> 00:16:27.230
to pick out all different kinds
of surfaces, to see colors,

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:30.930
to see shapes, and so forth.

00:16:30.930 --> 00:16:35.930
But when they face the task
of determining where they are

00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:37.830
and what paths they should take

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:38.920
to get from where they are

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:40.940
to somewhere else in the environment,

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:43.860
they disregard almost
all of this information

00:16:43.860 --> 00:16:45.760
and rely on only one thing,

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:48.820
which is the ground surface
over which they move

00:16:48.820 --> 00:16:50.860
and its perturbations.

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:54.170
That's an example of a
limit on a core system,

00:16:54.170 --> 00:16:57.503
a limit on the things that
it serves to represent.

00:16:58.520 --> 00:17:00.193
In addition to being limited,

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:04.840
the representations from the
core systems are shared...

00:17:04.840 --> 00:17:06.910
When we look at the abstract properties

00:17:06.910 --> 00:17:10.010
that characterize them and
the limits for each system,

00:17:10.010 --> 00:17:13.130
we get evidence that they're
shared by other animals,

00:17:13.130 --> 00:17:15.347
that it's not only the
case that we see objects

00:17:15.347 --> 00:17:17.590
and that other animals see objects,

00:17:17.590 --> 00:17:20.530
but we see objects under
the same conditions

00:17:20.530 --> 00:17:23.830
and with the same by using
the same abstract properties

00:17:23.830 --> 00:17:24.870
that other animals do,

00:17:24.870 --> 00:17:27.730
and with the same limits
on what we represent

00:17:27.730 --> 00:17:29.840
that we find in other animals.

00:17:29.840 --> 00:17:31.700
Now that gives us reason to believe that

00:17:31.700 --> 00:17:33.070
these systems are ancient,

00:17:33.070 --> 00:17:36.380
that they evolved long before we emerged

00:17:36.380 --> 00:17:39.470
as a species in ancestors
that are coming to us

00:17:39.470 --> 00:17:41.400
and to other animals.

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:44.890
Now, because they're ancient,

00:17:44.890 --> 00:17:46.670
they can be studied in animals,

00:17:46.670 --> 00:17:49.197
not only at a behavioral
level, but at a neural level.

00:17:49.197 --> 00:17:52.270
And this of course has been
done famously and richly

00:17:52.270 --> 00:17:54.690
in the case of the place system,

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:56.120
providing evidence that

00:17:56.120 --> 00:17:59.290
navigation relies on
specialized neural mechanisms

00:17:59.290 --> 00:18:03.480
in the hippocampus and surrounding cortex

00:18:03.480 --> 00:18:07.820
that these mechanisms
are present in animals

00:18:07.820 --> 00:18:08.870
throughout their lives.

00:18:08.870 --> 00:18:10.850
They were found first in mature animals,

00:18:10.850 --> 00:18:14.060
but then also tracked down in development

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:16.020
and found in animals
at the very beginning,

00:18:16.020 --> 00:18:19.040
just beginning to navigate
through the environment.

00:18:19.040 --> 00:18:20.700
Armed with this information,

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:22.320
functional brain imaging studies

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:25.080
revealed the same mechanisms in humans

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:29.320
providing yet more evidence
that we share common systems

00:18:29.320 --> 00:18:32.040
with these other animals,

00:18:32.040 --> 00:18:34.500
and also providing
evidence that in humans,

00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:36.190
as well as other animals,

00:18:36.190 --> 00:18:38.720
these systems are present throughout life.

00:18:38.720 --> 00:18:41.620
They're not scaffolding
that you use as an infant

00:18:41.620 --> 00:18:43.500
to get cognitive development going

00:18:43.500 --> 00:18:46.560
and then throw away as you latch on to

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:49.540
other kinds of concepts
and representations.

00:18:49.540 --> 00:18:53.560
Rather, they continue to function
in us throughout our lives

00:18:53.560 --> 00:18:56.030
and in other species as well.

00:18:56.030 --> 00:19:00.350
Now, because these systems
continue to function in adults.

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:03.470
You can do experiments like
some of the gorgeous experiments

00:19:03.470 --> 00:19:05.853
that Brian did generations ago.

00:19:06.820 --> 00:19:10.380
You can do experiments where
you catch them in action

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:12.053
in adult minds.

00:19:12.950 --> 00:19:16.360
And when you do this,
if you then ask people,

00:19:16.360 --> 00:19:19.300
what are you thinking about
while you're in this experiment?

00:19:19.300 --> 00:19:22.910
You discover that these systems
are operating automatically

00:19:22.910 --> 00:19:27.380
and effortlessly and unconsciously,

00:19:27.380 --> 00:19:30.090
but you also discover
that different systems,

00:19:30.090 --> 00:19:32.050
representations from different systems

00:19:32.050 --> 00:19:34.070
compete with one another for attention.

00:19:34.070 --> 00:19:37.380
So again, to use the place
system as an example,

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:40.370
when adults are in a
virtual navigation task,

00:19:40.370 --> 00:19:42.780
so they're navigating on a,
you know, with a joystick,

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:45.763
looking at a computer screen
through some environment,

00:19:47.071 --> 00:19:51.730
we're able to navigate, to
find our way to an object

00:19:51.730 --> 00:19:53.440
in at least two different ways.

00:19:53.440 --> 00:19:57.000
One, by using objects in
the environment as landmarks

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:59.550
and marking where we are relative to them,

00:19:59.550 --> 00:20:02.140
and two, by using the ground surface over

00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:06.850
and its perturbations
over which we're moving.

00:20:06.850 --> 00:20:08.320
Now, what you find in adults

00:20:08.320 --> 00:20:10.620
is we can use both of these systems,

00:20:10.620 --> 00:20:13.760
but on any given occasion, as we use one,

00:20:13.760 --> 00:20:15.620
it will tend to suppress the activity

00:20:15.620 --> 00:20:18.040
and the neural mechanisms
that support the other system.

00:20:18.040 --> 00:20:20.060
So you see a competition between them,

00:20:20.060 --> 00:20:22.280
and oh, and by the way,
if you then ask adults,

00:20:22.280 --> 00:20:23.480
what were you doing on that trial?

00:20:23.480 --> 00:20:26.040
And was it any different from
what you did on other trials?

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:28.500
They generally cannot tell you.

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:30.680
It's not that we're
consciously making decisions

00:20:30.680 --> 00:20:32.800
that we're gonna rely on
one system or the other,

00:20:32.800 --> 00:20:34.700
it's that they're automatically activated,

00:20:34.700 --> 00:20:37.920
but in the competitive
resource limited manner.

00:20:37.920 --> 00:20:40.360
Finally, this system
support children's learning

00:20:40.360 --> 00:20:41.940
in the case of the spa,

00:20:41.940 --> 00:20:43.910
the navigation system

00:20:43.910 --> 00:20:47.270
learning the layout of
their local environment,

00:20:47.270 --> 00:20:50.120
where things are and how you
get from one place to another.

00:20:50.120 --> 00:20:54.460
And there's some evidence in
the case of the place system

00:20:54.460 --> 00:20:56.200
that this learning,

00:20:56.200 --> 00:20:58.850
and also decision making
about which path to take

00:20:58.850 --> 00:21:03.010
through the layout are heavily
influenced by a capacity

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:04.950
that's been found both in adult animals

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:07.920
and also in young animals

00:21:09.088 --> 00:21:13.850
for mental simulations of
travel through the environment.

00:21:13.850 --> 00:21:16.060
So when an animal
confronts a choice point,

00:21:16.060 --> 00:21:18.960
a new choice point that it
hasn't confronted before

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:21.430
of taking either of two different paths

00:21:21.430 --> 00:21:22.990
to get to a goal object,

00:21:22.990 --> 00:21:25.470
it may well stop at that choice point

00:21:25.470 --> 00:21:27.870
and simulate one path or the other path,

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:29.340
and you can track these simulations

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:32.170
if you're monitoring
what activity is going on

00:21:33.210 --> 00:21:34.410
in their hippocampus.

00:21:34.410 --> 00:21:39.410
And then the ultimate decision
that the animal makes will,

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:42.020
the effectiveness of that
decision can be linked

00:21:42.020 --> 00:21:44.550
to the effectiveness of the simulations

00:21:44.550 --> 00:21:45.900
that it had engaged in.

00:21:45.900 --> 00:21:49.090
And that same process has also
been found in young animals

00:21:49.090 --> 00:21:51.530
and also in human adults.

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:53.800
So in the case of place representations,

00:21:53.800 --> 00:21:56.800
there is evidence that
processes of mental simulation

00:21:57.670 --> 00:22:01.940
enter into our learning about
the layout of the environment.

00:22:01.940 --> 00:22:04.360
The reason I qualified
this conclusion here

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:05.290
is that to my knowledge,

00:22:05.290 --> 00:22:08.920
there is no evidence
for any direct evidence

00:22:08.920 --> 00:22:11.680
for mental simulations in human infants.

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:15.720
So that remains an untested hypothesis.

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:17.360
But the last general thing I wanna say

00:22:17.360 --> 00:22:21.090
before plunging into
some more details is that

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:24.160
these different properties
of core knowledge systems

00:22:24.160 --> 00:22:27.670
are not just a random
laundry list of properties

00:22:27.670 --> 00:22:29.440
that have collected over time.

00:22:29.440 --> 00:22:32.350
I think they're interestingly
related to each other.

00:22:32.350 --> 00:22:35.280
And that any system that
has some of these properties

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:38.810
is highly likely to have all of them.

00:22:38.810 --> 00:22:39.643
And to see this,

00:22:39.643 --> 00:22:44.643
let's start with the property
of the ancient origins

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:45.700
of these systems.

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:47.820
And think back to creatures

00:22:47.820 --> 00:22:50.620
who lived hundreds of
millions of years ago,

00:22:50.620 --> 00:22:54.490
common ancestors to us
and some of the fish

00:22:54.490 --> 00:22:57.350
or maybe even fruit flies in some cases

00:22:57.350 --> 00:23:00.160
and other animals who
navigate through the world

00:23:00.160 --> 00:23:02.030
in some of the same ways that we do.

00:23:02.030 --> 00:23:04.820
And imagine that this
ancestor lived in the water

00:23:05.703 --> 00:23:08.360
and fed on a certain kind of food,

00:23:08.360 --> 00:23:11.680
and the environment in which
it lived lasted long enough

00:23:11.680 --> 00:23:15.230
that it was able to
evolve multiple mechanisms

00:23:15.230 --> 00:23:17.970
for guiding itself to the sources of food.

00:23:17.970 --> 00:23:21.620
First of all, the sources
occurred in predictable locations.

00:23:21.620 --> 00:23:24.150
So they kept by representing
their own position

00:23:24.150 --> 00:23:26.900
and the distances and
directions of those locations

00:23:26.900 --> 00:23:29.270
from their home position,

00:23:29.270 --> 00:23:31.730
they were able to use the
geometry of the layout

00:23:31.730 --> 00:23:33.433
to navigate to food.

00:23:34.270 --> 00:23:37.810
But the food also probably
had a characteristic odor,

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:41.150
chemical composition, which
might have created gradients

00:23:41.150 --> 00:23:42.980
in the water that they
were swimming through.

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:45.590
And they may well have
evolved other mechanisms

00:23:45.590 --> 00:23:47.800
for detecting the food visually

00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:49.880
or detecting the chemical gradients.

00:23:49.880 --> 00:23:52.010
But now think about their descendants.

00:23:52.010 --> 00:23:55.200
Over subsequent evolution,
the environments changed

00:23:55.200 --> 00:23:56.530
and the animals changed.

00:23:56.530 --> 00:23:58.560
Some of them grew bigger,
they grew smaller.

00:23:58.560 --> 00:23:59.860
Their energy requirements

00:23:59.860 --> 00:24:01.530
were no longer met by that kind of food.

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:03.610
They had other kinds of food.

00:24:03.610 --> 00:24:06.540
So any mechanisms that
were specific to the smell

00:24:06.540 --> 00:24:10.160
or the site or the chemical
composition of that food

00:24:10.160 --> 00:24:12.870
would be no longer
relevant to the descendants

00:24:12.870 --> 00:24:16.830
from that ancestors on
the way to getting to us.

00:24:16.830 --> 00:24:19.823
But for all of those intermediate species,

00:24:20.974 --> 00:24:23.880
the euclidean structure of the environment

00:24:23.880 --> 00:24:25.110
would've been the same.

00:24:25.110 --> 00:24:27.820
The mechanism for
navigating by the distances

00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:30.820
and directions of food
sources from the animal

00:24:30.820 --> 00:24:33.120
would be useful no matter what environment

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:34.720
that animal was in.

00:24:34.720 --> 00:24:39.720
So any system that was ancient
is very likely to focus on

00:24:39.800 --> 00:24:42.210
properties of the world that are abstract,

00:24:42.210 --> 00:24:45.690
that apply in all environments,
and that are limited,

00:24:45.690 --> 00:24:48.570
that don't apply to things
like what stuff looks like,

00:24:48.570 --> 00:24:49.560
what it smells like,

00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:52.350
the superficial properties
of things that differ

00:24:52.350 --> 00:24:54.720
from one environment to another.

00:24:54.720 --> 00:24:59.510
But if a system captures
abstract properties,

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:03.450
those properties are
gonna be extremely useful

00:25:03.450 --> 00:25:05.240
if they apply in all environments.

00:25:05.240 --> 00:25:08.250
And they're gonna imply not
only in all environments,

00:25:08.250 --> 00:25:11.210
but to people of all ages,

00:25:11.210 --> 00:25:13.270
even as people change their environment,

00:25:13.270 --> 00:25:15.850
if their lifestyle brings about, you know,

00:25:15.850 --> 00:25:19.900
they migrate or something, they
travel to new environments.

00:25:19.900 --> 00:25:21.970
These basic abstract
properties of the world

00:25:21.970 --> 00:25:24.000
will apply in all of those environments,

00:25:24.000 --> 00:25:27.150
from which it follows that these systems,

00:25:27.150 --> 00:25:28.520
if they emerge in infants,

00:25:28.520 --> 00:25:30.880
are very likely to stay
present and functional

00:25:34.092 --> 00:25:37.070
throughout our lives and
support our subsequent learning

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:38.460
and our subsequent reasoning

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:42.690
no matter what problems we
find ourselves confronted with.

00:25:42.690 --> 00:25:44.080
What's more,

00:25:44.080 --> 00:25:47.230
the systems are likely to
be neurally specialized

00:25:47.230 --> 00:25:51.310
and to operate automatically
and unconsciously,

00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:54.370
because if you have a system
that works in every environment

00:25:54.370 --> 00:25:55.940
that you're ever gonna be in,

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:57.500
then you don't need to waste

00:25:57.500 --> 00:26:00.790
your conscious decision making
resources and figuring out

00:26:00.790 --> 00:26:02.400
should I be using it or not?

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:03.880
Yes, you should always be using it.

00:26:03.880 --> 00:26:06.250
You should be using it automatically.

00:26:06.250 --> 00:26:09.850
However, there is a
problem, which is that,

00:26:09.850 --> 00:26:12.070
I guess I didn't put this
property on the list,

00:26:12.070 --> 00:26:15.410
but there is a problem that
we have multiple such systems,

00:26:15.410 --> 00:26:17.710
but we can only do one thing at a time.

00:26:17.710 --> 00:26:19.640
So I think it makes
sense that these systems,

00:26:19.640 --> 00:26:22.660
although they're automatic
and operate unconsciously,

00:26:22.660 --> 00:26:24.500
nevertheless, compete for resources

00:26:24.500 --> 00:26:25.780
so that animals aren't pulled in

00:26:25.780 --> 00:26:30.143
multiple different directions
at any given moment in time.

00:26:31.200 --> 00:26:32.033
And then finally,

00:26:32.033 --> 00:26:33.880
I think these systems may serve

00:26:35.297 --> 00:26:38.960
to simulate the entities
that populate the world

00:26:39.990 --> 00:26:43.750
that we find in these systems

00:26:43.750 --> 00:26:46.580
because they're supporting our learning

00:26:46.580 --> 00:26:49.553
automatically and effortlessly.

00:26:50.820 --> 00:26:53.530
They're able to do this
from the very beginning,

00:26:53.530 --> 00:26:55.410
since they're focusing
on abstract properties

00:26:55.410 --> 00:26:57.280
that don't depend on what
things are particularly

00:26:57.280 --> 00:27:00.690
gonna look like in one
environment relative to another.

00:27:00.690 --> 00:27:04.220
This could be a useful
way to prepare animals

00:27:04.220 --> 00:27:06.700
for the world that they're
gonna be encountering.

00:27:06.700 --> 00:27:08.900
So that's all the conclusions of the talk.

00:27:08.900 --> 00:27:11.250
Now, I can actually get to
the beginning of the talk

00:27:11.250 --> 00:27:12.383
and thank you.

00:27:13.420 --> 00:27:18.420
And what I wanna do is focus
on three of these systems

00:27:18.530 --> 00:27:21.220
to set up the second part of the talk

00:27:21.220 --> 00:27:24.090
where I ask how children learn.

00:27:24.090 --> 00:27:25.910
I think a lot of how children learn

00:27:25.910 --> 00:27:28.760
depends on the ways in which as infants,

00:27:28.760 --> 00:27:32.360
they represent objects and
agents and social beings.

00:27:32.360 --> 00:27:33.750
Now, I was actually here,

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:38.750
and some of you heard me talk
here before the pandemic.

00:27:39.160 --> 00:27:40.950
So I wanna go very quickly

00:27:40.950 --> 00:27:43.930
over stuff that we already knew back then.

00:27:43.930 --> 00:27:45.180
I'll they talk very quickly

00:27:45.180 --> 00:27:47.070
about what infants know about objects,

00:27:47.070 --> 00:27:48.970
but then tell you about some
new work we've been doing

00:27:48.970 --> 00:27:52.130
on infants representations
of agents and social beings,

00:27:52.130 --> 00:27:54.563
but not saliva sharing,
Brian, sorry about that.

00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:57.400
Okay, objects.

00:27:57.400 --> 00:28:00.130
So what do babies know about objects?

00:28:00.130 --> 00:28:01.750
Only really abstract things.

00:28:01.750 --> 00:28:03.110
They don't know what
they're gonna look like.

00:28:03.110 --> 00:28:04.610
They don't know what kinda categories

00:28:04.610 --> 00:28:06.050
they're going to fall into,

00:28:06.050 --> 00:28:06.883
but they know that out there in the world,

00:28:06.883 --> 00:28:10.910
there're going to be bodies
that move as cohesive holes

00:28:12.211 --> 00:28:14.950
that are fully interconnected.

00:28:14.950 --> 00:28:16.620
So if parts of them are moving together,

00:28:16.620 --> 00:28:20.170
there's gonna be connections
between those parts

00:28:20.170 --> 00:28:22.483
even if they aren't directly visible.

00:28:23.490 --> 00:28:27.710
And that move on unique
connected paths through space

00:28:27.710 --> 00:28:28.543
and over time.

00:28:28.543 --> 00:28:31.510
So if an object appears
behind the left screen,

00:28:31.510 --> 00:28:32.510
and then an object

00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:35.530
disappears behind the left screen,

00:28:35.530 --> 00:28:37.210
and then an object comes into view

00:28:37.210 --> 00:28:38.340
from behind the right screen

00:28:38.340 --> 00:28:41.570
but there was no object
moving between the two,

00:28:41.570 --> 00:28:43.730
infants can conclude that
there were two objects

00:28:43.730 --> 00:28:45.170
participating in that event.

00:28:45.170 --> 00:28:48.063
And if a screen comes
up and covers an object,

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:52.580
infants can predict that that screen,

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:53.640
it's rotating backward.

00:28:53.640 --> 00:28:55.480
Infants can predict that that screen

00:28:55.480 --> 00:28:58.310
should stop its rotation
when it reaches the point

00:28:58.310 --> 00:28:59.990
where that object is located,

00:28:59.990 --> 00:29:01.880
even though it's no longer visible

00:29:01.880 --> 00:29:04.140
'cause two things can't be
in the same place at once.

00:29:04.140 --> 00:29:08.420
And if one object that
red block moves toward

00:29:10.205 --> 00:29:11.850
a blue cylinder, that's partly,

00:29:11.850 --> 00:29:13.980
that's initially
stationary behind a screen,

00:29:13.980 --> 00:29:15.560
and then after it disappears,

00:29:15.560 --> 00:29:17.050
the blue cylinder starts to move,

00:29:17.050 --> 00:29:18.610
infants infer that the block

00:29:18.610 --> 00:29:20.620
will have contacted the cylinder

00:29:20.620 --> 00:29:23.330
that they interacted on contact.

00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:27.400
Now, interestingly research
by Susan Carey has shown that

00:29:27.400 --> 00:29:28.480
if one of these prop,

00:29:28.480 --> 00:29:31.510
it's not that infants
can't tolerate violations

00:29:31.510 --> 00:29:33.720
of these properties, you can violate them.

00:29:33.720 --> 00:29:35.730
But if you violate one
of these properties,

00:29:35.730 --> 00:29:38.330
they suspend all their
predictions about the others.

00:29:38.330 --> 00:29:40.990
So the properties seem
to be interconnected.

00:29:40.990 --> 00:29:42.970
These aren't just separate things

00:29:42.970 --> 00:29:45.684
that infants know about objects.

00:29:45.684 --> 00:29:50.530
They're connected together and
they rise and fall together.

00:29:50.530 --> 00:29:53.290
And all of these abilities
they've been studied in infants

00:29:53.290 --> 00:29:55.260
at a variety of different ages.

00:29:55.260 --> 00:29:57.980
They've been rebuild in human
infants at different ages.

00:29:57.980 --> 00:30:01.260
And very few of them have been
tested in newborn infants,

00:30:01.260 --> 00:30:02.870
but all of them have been tested

00:30:02.870 --> 00:30:06.160
in controlled reared
animals of various species.

00:30:06.160 --> 00:30:08.040
And when tested,

00:30:08.040 --> 00:30:10.240
they've been found to be
present and functional

00:30:10.240 --> 00:30:11.790
on an animal's first encounter,

00:30:11.790 --> 00:30:15.810
perceptual encounter with an object.

00:30:15.810 --> 00:30:18.470
Okay, but these abilities
are extremely limited,

00:30:18.470 --> 00:30:20.060
and I'm gonna mention just one limit

00:30:20.060 --> 00:30:22.910
from a classic experiment
conducted 25 years ago,

00:30:22.910 --> 00:30:27.570
or set of experiments by
Fay Xu and Susan Carey

00:30:27.570 --> 00:30:29.030
with 10 month old infants.

00:30:29.030 --> 00:30:31.560
These were studies in which
infants were shown objects

00:30:31.560 --> 00:30:33.250
that very likely were familiar to them

00:30:33.250 --> 00:30:36.720
like a shoe, baby's shoe,
that went behind a screen

00:30:36.720 --> 00:30:39.170
and a rubber ducky that came out

00:30:39.170 --> 00:30:41.870
from the other side of the screen.

00:30:41.870 --> 00:30:43.940
And after seeing that event repeatedly,

00:30:43.940 --> 00:30:45.170
they were asked in effect

00:30:45.170 --> 00:30:47.360
how many objects are behind the screen

00:30:47.360 --> 00:30:49.920
by taking the screen away
and alternating trials

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:52.743
revealing either both
objects or only one of them,

00:30:54.939 --> 00:30:57.320
measuring how long infants who hadn't seen

00:30:57.320 --> 00:31:01.200
the previous events looked
at those two displays

00:31:01.200 --> 00:31:03.450
and comparing that to how long the infants

00:31:03.450 --> 00:31:05.620
who saw the previous
events looked at them.

00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:10.620
What they found is that the
previous events in no way

00:31:10.730 --> 00:31:12.620
disposed the infants to conclude that

00:31:12.620 --> 00:31:14.240
there were two objects behind the screen,

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:15.860
they seemed completely open-minded

00:31:15.860 --> 00:31:18.360
about whether there were two
objects behind that screen

00:31:18.360 --> 00:31:21.350
or one shoe that turned itself into a duck

00:31:21.350 --> 00:31:24.380
during the time that it was hidden.

00:31:24.380 --> 00:31:25.670
Now you could think, well,

00:31:25.670 --> 00:31:27.300
maybe this is just a memory problem,

00:31:27.300 --> 00:31:28.900
the kids weren't keeping track

00:31:28.900 --> 00:31:31.470
of the appearance of the objects,

00:31:31.470 --> 00:31:34.210
but Xu and Carey showed
that that wasn't true

00:31:34.210 --> 00:31:36.220
because if you measured
how interested they were

00:31:36.220 --> 00:31:38.290
in the events where the shoe and the duck

00:31:38.290 --> 00:31:40.720
ultimately appeared
relative to events where

00:31:40.720 --> 00:31:42.760
the duck appeared on
both sides of the screen

00:31:42.760 --> 00:31:45.030
or the shoe appeared on
both sides of the screen,

00:31:45.030 --> 00:31:46.160
they were more interested

00:31:46.160 --> 00:31:48.320
when there was a change in features.

00:31:48.320 --> 00:31:51.890
Also in tasks involving no memory demands

00:31:51.890 --> 00:31:53.880
where the question for the infants is not

00:31:53.880 --> 00:31:56.660
what's happening when these
things are out of you,

00:31:56.660 --> 00:31:59.040
but rather if I lift the duck,

00:31:59.040 --> 00:32:00.350
what's gonna happen to the truck?

00:32:00.350 --> 00:32:02.560
Will it come with it or
will it stay on the table?

00:32:02.560 --> 00:32:03.930
Are these one connected body

00:32:03.930 --> 00:32:05.540
or are they two separate bodies?

00:32:05.540 --> 00:32:08.980
The infants were neutral
between those two possibilities.

00:32:08.980 --> 00:32:11.360
Even as many of them demonstrated that

00:32:11.360 --> 00:32:13.640
they recognized the duck
by saying quack, quack,

00:32:13.640 --> 00:32:16.640
while they looked at this display
or the truck and so forth.

00:32:16.640 --> 00:32:19.370
So it looks like infants
can recognize some objects

00:32:19.370 --> 00:32:21.570
and even imitate their functions

00:32:21.570 --> 00:32:24.960
without using that information
to answer the question,

00:32:24.960 --> 00:32:27.950
where does this object end
and the other one begin,

00:32:27.950 --> 00:32:29.780
to track objects over time.

00:32:29.780 --> 00:32:32.660
So this suggests that when
reasoning about object motion,

00:32:32.660 --> 00:32:35.260
infants are representing
objects as bodies,

00:32:35.260 --> 00:32:38.930
but not as kinds with
characteristic forms and functions.

00:32:38.930 --> 00:32:40.880
I think a way of thinking about this is

00:32:42.280 --> 00:32:44.440
this is a system that's telling infants

00:32:44.440 --> 00:32:47.700
what individual things are
out there in the world,

00:32:47.700 --> 00:32:50.010
but not how should these
things be categorized

00:32:50.010 --> 00:32:51.130
and what generalizations

00:32:51.130 --> 00:32:54.190
should I form over those categories?

00:32:54.190 --> 00:32:55.670
And here, I think there's a parallel

00:32:55.670 --> 00:32:58.520
with the physics engines
that Josh 10 and bound

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:00.710
may have talked about when he was here

00:33:00.710 --> 00:33:04.800
in which I know some of you
have been interested in,

00:33:04.800 --> 00:33:09.130
or working on that simulate
object motions and interactions.

00:33:09.130 --> 00:33:12.570
But let me move on to some stuff

00:33:12.570 --> 00:33:15.130
that we're working on actively now.

00:33:15.130 --> 00:33:20.130
Infants early developing
understanding of people as agents.

00:33:21.060 --> 00:33:23.440
So over the last 25 years or so,

00:33:23.440 --> 00:33:25.410
we've learned a fair amount about,

00:33:25.410 --> 00:33:28.440
people have learned a fair
amount about infants expectations

00:33:28.440 --> 00:33:31.800
about how agents will and will not act.

00:33:31.800 --> 00:33:34.470
We've learned that agents
tend to act efficiently

00:33:34.470 --> 00:33:35.820
to achieve their goals.

00:33:35.820 --> 00:33:38.400
So if an agent consistently
reaches for an object

00:33:38.400 --> 00:33:41.150
over a barrier and then
the barrier is removed,

00:33:41.150 --> 00:33:43.770
they'll change the
trajectory of their reach

00:33:43.770 --> 00:33:47.300
to the more efficient direct
reach for that object.

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:51.040
We also know that infants
will take higher cost actions

00:33:51.040 --> 00:33:53.870
to attain goals of greater value.

00:33:53.870 --> 00:33:56.610
So if infants see that
the little red agent

00:33:56.610 --> 00:34:00.420
is willing to climb a steep
hill to get to the yellow guy,

00:34:00.420 --> 00:34:03.200
but only a shallow hill
to get to the blue guy.

00:34:03.200 --> 00:34:05.210
Then when the two, the
yellow and blue guys

00:34:05.210 --> 00:34:07.310
both appear on a level playing field,

00:34:07.310 --> 00:34:09.600
they expect the agent
to go to the one that

00:34:09.600 --> 00:34:12.550
they had taken a more costly
action to get to previously.

00:34:12.550 --> 00:34:15.130
And finally, we know that

00:34:15.130 --> 00:34:18.450
babies expect that
agents will act on things

00:34:18.450 --> 00:34:20.873
that are perceptually accessible to them.

00:34:22.050 --> 00:34:24.340
And when they do,

00:34:24.340 --> 00:34:26.890
their actions will reveal
their relative value

00:34:26.890 --> 00:34:29.150
that they give to each of these objects.

00:34:29.150 --> 00:34:33.540
So this is worked by you
Yuon, Luo and Susan Johnson,

00:34:33.540 --> 00:34:36.540
in which on the two top lines,

00:34:36.540 --> 00:34:39.190
you see experiments in which

00:34:39.190 --> 00:34:42.090
an agent can see each of two objects

00:34:42.090 --> 00:34:43.690
and reaches for one of them.

00:34:43.690 --> 00:34:45.210
And under those conditions,

00:34:45.210 --> 00:34:47.600
infants infer that the agent prefers

00:34:47.600 --> 00:34:49.540
that object to the other object,

00:34:49.540 --> 00:34:52.740
contrasted with two conditions in which

00:34:52.740 --> 00:34:55.370
the agent can only see
one of the two objects

00:34:55.370 --> 00:34:57.720
either because the other is
fully hidden behind a screen

00:34:57.720 --> 00:35:00.370
or because their back is
turned to the other object.

00:35:00.370 --> 00:35:01.610
And in that condition,

00:35:01.610 --> 00:35:02.960
they make no inference about

00:35:02.960 --> 00:35:06.130
whether the agent prefers the
object that she reaches for

00:35:06.130 --> 00:35:07.150
to the other object.

00:35:07.150 --> 00:35:08.160
Only when she can see it,

00:35:08.160 --> 00:35:10.490
do they think that that
there's a preference.

00:35:10.490 --> 00:35:14.570
Now, these properties
also connect to each other

00:35:14.570 --> 00:35:16.110
inferences about preferences

00:35:16.110 --> 00:35:18.640
connecting to inferences about visibility,

00:35:18.640 --> 00:35:21.560
inferences connecting also to inferences

00:35:21.560 --> 00:35:24.770
about the amount of cost
you would take for an action

00:35:24.770 --> 00:35:26.130
and so forth.

00:35:26.130 --> 00:35:29.110
And they also connect to a
further abstract property,

00:35:29.110 --> 00:35:32.720
namely that agents actions
cause changes in the world.

00:35:32.720 --> 00:35:35.030
And for this, I wanna talk
very quickly about a study

00:35:35.030 --> 00:35:38.540
that I talked about at much
more length when I was here

00:35:38.540 --> 00:35:40.330
three years ago

00:35:40.330 --> 00:35:41.963
that showed that babies,

00:35:42.980 --> 00:35:47.220
when infants of about three months of age,

00:35:47.220 --> 00:35:50.290
that is infants who are not
yet able to reach for objects

00:35:50.290 --> 00:35:53.490
and pick them up or act
on them systematically,

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:55.870
basically all a three
month old baby can do

00:35:55.870 --> 00:35:59.220
if you show them an object
within near reaching distances,

00:35:59.220 --> 00:36:00.620
they kinda go like this and look like

00:36:00.620 --> 00:36:02.640
they would really like you
to give them the object,

00:36:02.640 --> 00:36:04.460
but they can't actually get it themselves.

00:36:04.460 --> 00:36:05.710
And if you move it close enough

00:36:05.710 --> 00:36:07.080
that they might bump into it,

00:36:07.080 --> 00:36:09.050
they actually bat it away, okay?

00:36:09.050 --> 00:36:10.420
They can't actually reach,

00:36:10.420 --> 00:36:12.750
engage in a coordinated
reach for an object.

00:36:12.750 --> 00:36:15.320
So we take infants of that age and ask

00:36:15.320 --> 00:36:19.300
if you show them another
person acting on an object

00:36:19.300 --> 00:36:22.180
and causing a change in
that object on contact,

00:36:22.180 --> 00:36:24.440
do they see that as a
goal-directed action?

00:36:24.440 --> 00:36:27.180
And in particular, will they
infer that it'll be efficient?

00:36:27.180 --> 00:36:31.300
So in this condition,
Sherry Liu showed a person,

00:36:31.300 --> 00:36:34.300
actually, she used to be here
as a postdoc at one point,

00:36:34.300 --> 00:36:37.010
Elliot Martin, reaching over a barrier,

00:36:37.010 --> 00:36:39.080
touching an object and
the object lights up

00:36:39.080 --> 00:36:40.300
and makes a noise.

00:36:40.300 --> 00:36:44.150
And then the barrier is removed

00:36:44.150 --> 00:36:46.451
and the infant see a direct reach

00:36:46.451 --> 00:36:48.787
and indirect reach
alternately for the object.

00:36:48.787 --> 00:36:51.130
And the question is which
did they expect to see?

00:36:51.130 --> 00:36:52.950
And she compared this condition

00:36:52.950 --> 00:36:56.200
to a condition that was made
with exactly the same videos,

00:36:56.200 --> 00:36:58.700
differing only in one half second.

00:36:58.700 --> 00:37:01.130
For this condition, the video was modified

00:37:01.130 --> 00:37:04.060
so that the hands stopped
short of the object

00:37:04.060 --> 00:37:05.390
and froze in that position,

00:37:05.390 --> 00:37:08.710
and then after a half second
gap in time, the object lit up.

00:37:08.710 --> 00:37:12.000
So no space temporal
contiguity between the action

00:37:12.000 --> 00:37:14.420
and the effect on the object.

00:37:14.420 --> 00:37:17.180
And what she found was that
in the contact condition,

00:37:17.180 --> 00:37:19.440
the babies expected the efficient reach

00:37:19.440 --> 00:37:21.720
in the no contact condition.

00:37:21.720 --> 00:37:24.750
They did not expect the efficient reach.

00:37:24.750 --> 00:37:26.880
They had no expectation.

00:37:26.880 --> 00:37:27.713
So it looks like

00:37:27.713 --> 00:37:30.110
before infants can pick
up an object themselves,

00:37:30.110 --> 00:37:33.610
they can strew other
people's actions as costly,

00:37:33.610 --> 00:37:37.090
but only if they also
see them as causal, okay?

00:37:37.090 --> 00:37:42.000
In the non causal condition
that that effect didn't occur.

00:37:42.000 --> 00:37:45.610
Now, those are the
inter-connected abstract concepts

00:37:45.610 --> 00:37:47.760
that we see in young infants.

00:37:47.760 --> 00:37:50.643
But there's a limit to young
infants agent knowledge of,

00:37:51.670 --> 00:37:54.790
their knowledge about
the actions of agents

00:37:54.790 --> 00:37:56.500
that was discovered by Amanda Woodward

00:37:56.500 --> 00:38:00.220
and Jessica Summerville that
we've been curious about.

00:38:00.220 --> 00:38:02.810
It comes up in a simple situation

00:38:02.810 --> 00:38:05.590
that works beautifully with
a five month old infant.

00:38:05.590 --> 00:38:06.920
Five month old infants, by the way,

00:38:06.920 --> 00:38:09.640
are now by then in the
business of picking up objects

00:38:09.640 --> 00:38:10.700
and manipulating them.

00:38:10.700 --> 00:38:13.180
And these infants are showing a person

00:38:13.180 --> 00:38:16.140
who consistently reaches
for one of two objects

00:38:16.140 --> 00:38:18.010
presented in constant positions,

00:38:18.010 --> 00:38:19.930
in this case, reaching for the ball

00:38:19.930 --> 00:38:22.300
which is always on the left.

00:38:22.300 --> 00:38:24.397
And then, Woodward asks,

00:38:24.397 --> 00:38:26.487
"How are babies understanding that reach?

00:38:26.487 --> 00:38:30.370
"How are they representing that action?"

00:38:30.370 --> 00:38:33.200
By allowing babies to see
that event repeatedly,

00:38:33.200 --> 00:38:36.450
and then reversing the
positions of the two objects

00:38:36.450 --> 00:38:39.810
and ultimately presenting
a reach on a novel path

00:38:39.810 --> 00:38:42.930
to a novel location, but
where the same object is

00:38:42.930 --> 00:38:47.750
versus a reach on the familiar
path now to a novel object.

00:38:47.750 --> 00:38:49.220
And at five months of age,

00:38:49.220 --> 00:38:52.730
babies expect the reach on the novel path

00:38:52.730 --> 00:38:53.900
to the familiar object

00:38:53.900 --> 00:38:56.100
and look longer at the other one, okay?

00:38:56.100 --> 00:38:59.380
So they see this reach
as directed to the ball

00:38:59.380 --> 00:39:00.330
in this case,

00:39:00.330 --> 00:39:03.200
not directed to a particular
location in space.

00:39:03.200 --> 00:39:04.520
That's at five months.

00:39:04.520 --> 00:39:07.250
At three months, they saw
something very different.

00:39:07.250 --> 00:39:09.070
The babies were equal,

00:39:09.070 --> 00:39:12.730
had no expectation about where the reach

00:39:12.730 --> 00:39:15.460
would be directed.

00:39:15.460 --> 00:39:18.200
Then later, they saw a
different development

00:39:18.200 --> 00:39:19.660
that also seemed to track

00:39:19.660 --> 00:39:22.370
the development of the
baby's own action capacities.

00:39:22.370 --> 00:39:25.480
This is a case where studied

00:39:25.480 --> 00:39:28.920
at length by (mumbles)
many generations earlier,

00:39:28.920 --> 00:39:31.280
where babies have to
do one thing like pull,

00:39:31.280 --> 00:39:32.990
they see somebody who does one thing,

00:39:32.990 --> 00:39:36.010
pulling on a cane in order
to create the conditions

00:39:36.010 --> 00:39:39.020
where they can do a second thing, grab,

00:39:39.020 --> 00:39:41.280
pick up an object that was
previously out of reach,

00:39:41.280 --> 00:39:43.180
but that the cane brought within reach.

00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:46.330
And when they presented this
event to 12 month old infants,

00:39:46.330 --> 00:39:49.580
the infants recognized
that the goal of the action

00:39:49.580 --> 00:39:52.630
was the toy that was out of reach.

00:39:52.630 --> 00:39:56.120
By that age, they're also
inclined to use canes

00:39:56.120 --> 00:39:59.910
to pull toys into their reaching space.

00:39:59.910 --> 00:40:02.320
But at eight months,
before they engage in that

00:40:03.350 --> 00:40:07.060
two step action, they took the
goal to be the cane itself,

00:40:07.060 --> 00:40:07.920
not to be the toy.

00:40:07.920 --> 00:40:09.760
So again, it looked like there was a limit

00:40:09.760 --> 00:40:13.230
to infants understanding
of other people's actions,

00:40:13.230 --> 00:40:15.700
and that the limits are
overcome at the point at which

00:40:15.700 --> 00:40:18.090
the infants themselves
master those actions.

00:40:18.090 --> 00:40:19.810
But this raises the question,

00:40:19.810 --> 00:40:21.750
how do infants understand actions

00:40:21.750 --> 00:40:23.640
that they can't yet perform?

00:40:23.640 --> 00:40:27.060
And what Woodward and
Somerville suggested is that

00:40:27.060 --> 00:40:28.580
they don't understand them.

00:40:28.580 --> 00:40:33.010
They see the behavior of
the agents in these studies

00:40:33.010 --> 00:40:36.140
as purposeless until they
get to the point where

00:40:36.140 --> 00:40:39.610
they themselves have and
can realize these purposes,

00:40:39.610 --> 00:40:42.740
and then they start endowing
other people with them.

00:40:42.740 --> 00:40:45.810
But we thought maybe we could
get young babies to succeed

00:40:45.810 --> 00:40:47.830
earlier in understanding that

00:40:47.830 --> 00:40:50.060
these actions were goal directed.

00:40:50.060 --> 00:40:52.550
And we now, as Brandon Woo and Shari Liu,

00:40:52.550 --> 00:40:54.310
and our first attempt to do this

00:40:54.310 --> 00:40:57.070
focused on the same Woodward situation

00:40:57.070 --> 00:40:59.910
that I just described with
three month old infants.

00:40:59.910 --> 00:41:01.650
And we just did one thing different.

00:41:01.650 --> 00:41:03.420
Based on the last study I told you about

00:41:03.420 --> 00:41:04.800
that Shari had performed,

00:41:04.800 --> 00:41:08.380
we had a person reach
for one of two objects,

00:41:08.380 --> 00:41:09.540
the bear or the toy,

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:11.570
but always consistently for one of them.

00:41:11.570 --> 00:41:14.320
But at the point at which the
person contacted that object,

00:41:14.320 --> 00:41:16.110
both objects lit up, okay?

00:41:16.110 --> 00:41:17.310
And the thinking was,

00:41:17.310 --> 00:41:20.410
well, we know that action
at a distance doesn't work.

00:41:20.410 --> 00:41:22.420
So touching this, the
babies shouldn't think that

00:41:22.420 --> 00:41:24.960
that will have a causal
effect on this object.

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:25.793
But they should think

00:41:25.793 --> 00:41:27.380
it'll have a causal effect on that one

00:41:27.380 --> 00:41:29.400
and maybe that would lead them to succeed

00:41:29.400 --> 00:41:31.280
in a Woodward experiment.

00:41:31.280 --> 00:41:34.690
So we then switched the locations
to see what would happen,

00:41:34.690 --> 00:41:37.100
and I fully expected that
this study would work.

00:41:37.100 --> 00:41:38.920
And this is the point at which,

00:41:38.920 --> 00:41:41.230
this is what I'm in
this business for, okay?

00:41:41.230 --> 00:41:43.030
I was dead wrong about that.

00:41:43.030 --> 00:41:45.080
The babies taught me something.

00:41:45.080 --> 00:41:48.880
They were looked absolutely
equally at the change in object

00:41:48.880 --> 00:41:50.520
and the change in location.

00:41:50.520 --> 00:41:54.070
They had no preference
for one over the other.

00:41:54.070 --> 00:41:58.320
So I needed to rethink
what was going on here.

00:41:58.320 --> 00:42:00.270
And it seemed like there
were two possibilities,

00:42:00.270 --> 00:42:01.200
probably maybe more than two,

00:42:01.200 --> 00:42:03.410
but these were the two that we focused on.

00:42:03.410 --> 00:42:06.350
One is the Woodward,
Summerville, possibility.

00:42:06.350 --> 00:42:09.040
Babies are just seeing these movement,

00:42:09.040 --> 00:42:11.280
the people as engaged in aimless,

00:42:11.280 --> 00:42:14.190
purposeless, goalless, movements, okay?

00:42:14.190 --> 00:42:16.640
But it seemed like there
was a second possibility,

00:42:16.640 --> 00:42:17.880
maybe in the same way that

00:42:17.880 --> 00:42:20.610
babies use core knowledge of objects

00:42:20.610 --> 00:42:25.610
to represent individual objects,
but not kinds of things.

00:42:25.830 --> 00:42:28.660
Maybe they use core
representations of agents

00:42:28.660 --> 00:42:31.010
to represent individual actions

00:42:31.010 --> 00:42:33.830
without understanding that
there are kinds of actions.

00:42:33.830 --> 00:42:36.470
There are acts of navigation
that serve to take you

00:42:36.470 --> 00:42:37.760
from place to place.

00:42:37.760 --> 00:42:40.180
And there are acts of manipulating objects

00:42:40.180 --> 00:42:43.160
that serve to get you
to particular objects

00:42:43.160 --> 00:42:44.620
that have particular affordances

00:42:44.620 --> 00:42:48.600
that you wanna capitalize on.

00:42:48.600 --> 00:42:51.080
Maybe they don't have that second level,

00:42:51.080 --> 00:42:54.870
in which case they might perfectly
well think of this person

00:42:54.870 --> 00:42:57.560
as acting in a goal-directed manner,

00:42:57.560 --> 00:42:59.860
but be open to the
possibility that that goal

00:42:59.860 --> 00:43:03.900
could be either an object or
a location in space, okay?

00:43:03.900 --> 00:43:05.720
So to distinguish those possibilities,

00:43:05.720 --> 00:43:08.560
Brandon and Shari ran
two more experiments.

00:43:08.560 --> 00:43:09.780
Here's one of them.

00:43:09.780 --> 00:43:13.070
This experiment involved
familiarizing infants

00:43:13.070 --> 00:43:16.863
with reaches to one object
at two different locations.

00:43:17.740 --> 00:43:20.210
So for some of the infants,

00:43:20.210 --> 00:43:22.630
here's what the familiarization was.

00:43:22.630 --> 00:43:26.070
This person always reached for the bear,

00:43:26.070 --> 00:43:27.450
but on alternating trials,

00:43:27.450 --> 00:43:29.580
the bear appeared in the
middle of the display

00:43:29.580 --> 00:43:31.380
or on the left side of the display.

00:43:31.380 --> 00:43:34.990
Across different babies, we
varied which object was the goal

00:43:34.990 --> 00:43:38.070
and whether it was on the left
or on the right and so forth.

00:43:38.070 --> 00:43:40.240
But they repeatedly saw that

00:43:40.240 --> 00:43:41.860
the guy was going for the object

00:43:41.860 --> 00:43:44.350
regardless of which of
two locations it was at.

00:43:44.350 --> 00:43:47.820
And then they were tested with that object

00:43:47.820 --> 00:43:50.950
in a completely new location
that had never appeared before

00:43:50.950 --> 00:43:53.510
versus the other object which
had never been reached for

00:43:53.510 --> 00:43:56.410
in one of the two familiar locations.

00:43:56.410 --> 00:44:00.490
So that's the Woodward situation,

00:44:00.490 --> 00:44:03.430
but with information that it's the object

00:44:03.430 --> 00:44:05.080
and not the location that matters.

00:44:05.080 --> 00:44:08.320
And in this case now, we
see the Woodward effect.

00:44:08.320 --> 00:44:12.310
We see the infants expecting
a reach to a new location

00:44:12.310 --> 00:44:15.570
for the familiar object
to be more similar to you,

00:44:15.570 --> 00:44:17.070
they deem that to be more similar

00:44:17.070 --> 00:44:18.310
to what they were seeing before

00:44:18.310 --> 00:44:19.670
and therefore less newsworthy,

00:44:19.670 --> 00:44:22.100
and so they look at it less long.

00:44:22.100 --> 00:44:24.330
Now that could be for
either of two reasons.

00:44:24.330 --> 00:44:25.960
They're three months old.

00:44:25.960 --> 00:44:29.080
They've been looking at
events in which other people

00:44:29.080 --> 00:44:31.630
reach for objects and pick
them up and manipulate them.

00:44:31.630 --> 00:44:33.780
That's how they get all
this nice motion information

00:44:33.780 --> 00:44:36.100
about objects most of the time.

00:44:36.100 --> 00:44:39.410
So they could be predisposed to learn that

00:44:39.410 --> 00:44:42.720
reaches tend to be directed
to objects, possibility one.

00:44:42.720 --> 00:44:46.220
Or possibility two, they could
be completely open-minded

00:44:46.220 --> 00:44:49.850
and be equally ready to learn
that reaches are directed

00:44:49.850 --> 00:44:51.430
to locations in space.

00:44:51.430 --> 00:44:53.880
So the last experiment tested for that.

00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:54.720
To test for that,

00:44:54.720 --> 00:44:57.980
we have to have reaches to one location,

00:44:57.980 --> 00:45:00.420
but two objects that can
appear at that location,

00:45:00.420 --> 00:45:02.410
and then we need a third novel object

00:45:02.410 --> 00:45:04.900
which is only gonna appear at test.

00:45:04.900 --> 00:45:09.753
And at test, you can trust
reach to a familiar object.

00:45:09.753 --> 00:45:12.610
I previously reached for
object at a new location

00:45:12.610 --> 00:45:16.630
with a new object at the old location.

00:45:16.630 --> 00:45:19.290
And now we see an effect at least as large

00:45:19.290 --> 00:45:22.350
of learning to expect
a reach to a location

00:45:22.350 --> 00:45:25.520
as learning to expect a
reach to an object, okay?

00:45:25.520 --> 00:45:29.680
So I conclude from this that
infants can quickly learn

00:45:29.680 --> 00:45:33.570
at three months that a
person's goal is either

00:45:33.570 --> 00:45:35.470
to get to a particular object

00:45:35.470 --> 00:45:38.350
or to get to a particular point in space.

00:45:38.350 --> 00:45:41.330
And what they learn depends
on the nature of the evidence

00:45:41.330 --> 00:45:42.380
that they're getting.

00:45:43.440 --> 00:45:46.830
But before they learn that,

00:45:46.830 --> 00:45:50.800
they represent other people's
actions as goal directed.

00:45:50.800 --> 00:45:52.390
They know this action has a goal.

00:45:52.390 --> 00:45:55.120
They just don't know
which of two goals it is,

00:45:55.120 --> 00:45:59.250
and they need to get
information about that.

00:45:59.250 --> 00:46:01.180
But there are limits to,

00:46:01.180 --> 00:46:04.420
interesting limits to infants knowledge.

00:46:04.420 --> 00:46:06.050
And I wanna talk about one,

00:46:06.050 --> 00:46:09.240
using experiments on 15 month old infants

00:46:09.240 --> 00:46:14.240
that gets to issues about
experience and mental states

00:46:15.090 --> 00:46:19.670
that I know from our
conversations today and yesterday

00:46:19.670 --> 00:46:22.450
that many of you are interested in,

00:46:22.450 --> 00:46:26.520
and also I'm puzzling over
these experimental results now.

00:46:26.520 --> 00:46:29.650
Okay, these are studies
with 15 month old infants.

00:46:29.650 --> 00:46:31.300
And the first of them,

00:46:31.300 --> 00:46:35.670
they both ask what do
infants understand about

00:46:35.670 --> 00:46:38.230
other people's mental states

00:46:38.230 --> 00:46:40.280
when those people are
viewing the same array

00:46:40.280 --> 00:46:42.990
that you're viewing but from
a different perspective?

00:46:42.990 --> 00:46:45.990
So here's a situation where
infants were first shown.

00:46:45.990 --> 00:46:47.390
You don't see this here,

00:46:47.390 --> 00:46:48.450
but they were first shown that

00:46:48.450 --> 00:46:50.920
there are two photographs of faces.

00:46:50.920 --> 00:46:52.230
By the way, all of these studies,

00:46:52.230 --> 00:46:53.650
including the ones I just told you about,

00:46:53.650 --> 00:46:56.130
were conducted over Zoom
during the pandemic.

00:46:56.130 --> 00:46:57.750
This is all new work.

00:46:57.750 --> 00:47:00.110
And most of them are not published yet.

00:47:00.110 --> 00:47:02.930
I'd love, you know,
people's reactions to them.

00:47:02.930 --> 00:47:05.680
But initially, they see
just the two photographs.

00:47:05.680 --> 00:47:08.450
So they're in view for
this person, this actor,

00:47:08.450 --> 00:47:10.820
and also in the infants view, of course.

00:47:10.820 --> 00:47:12.570
And then the occluders come down,

00:47:12.570 --> 00:47:14.210
and the occluders are placed

00:47:14.210 --> 00:47:17.950
so that one of the objects is
hidden from the baby's view

00:47:17.950 --> 00:47:21.100
but visible to the actor,
and the other is the reverse.

00:47:21.100 --> 00:47:25.690
And the actor now points
to either consistently

00:47:25.690 --> 00:47:27.860
to the object that he can see

00:47:27.860 --> 00:47:30.520
or to the screen covering
the object he can't see,

00:47:30.520 --> 00:47:31.930
which of those two he points to

00:47:31.930 --> 00:47:34.010
is counterbalanced across infants,

00:47:34.010 --> 00:47:35.880
but infants consistently see him doing one

00:47:35.880 --> 00:47:38.120
or the other of those actions.

00:47:38.120 --> 00:47:40.800
And the question is, how do
they represent that action?

00:47:40.800 --> 00:47:43.890
Do they represent it that
he's pointing to the screen

00:47:43.890 --> 00:47:44.800
that I'm looking at,

00:47:44.800 --> 00:47:47.410
or do they represent it as
he's pointing to the picture

00:47:47.410 --> 00:47:49.003
that he's looking at, okay?

00:47:50.020 --> 00:47:53.010
And to get at those two distinctions,

00:47:53.010 --> 00:47:54.550
after that familiarization,

00:47:54.550 --> 00:47:56.470
the person moves to the
other side of the room

00:47:56.470 --> 00:47:59.440
so his perspective and the
infant's perspective coincide.

00:47:59.440 --> 00:48:01.720
And now, depending on which perspective

00:48:01.720 --> 00:48:02.740
they attributed to him,

00:48:02.740 --> 00:48:06.460
they would see one or the
other of these events as novel.

00:48:06.460 --> 00:48:08.650
And in that situation,

00:48:08.650 --> 00:48:11.660
this is the event that corresponds
to what they saw before.

00:48:11.660 --> 00:48:15.170
They see this one as more
novel and look at it longer,

00:48:15.170 --> 00:48:19.700
providing evidence that
they do take account

00:48:19.700 --> 00:48:24.320
of the visual access of the
other person to the object.

00:48:24.320 --> 00:48:27.090
We show this in this study at 15 months,

00:48:27.090 --> 00:48:30.920
it had previously been
shown by Luo and Johnson

00:48:30.920 --> 00:48:33.230
at much younger ages,
at three months of age

00:48:33.230 --> 00:48:34.720
using a simpler situation,

00:48:34.720 --> 00:48:37.930
but logically testing
for the same ability.

00:48:37.930 --> 00:48:39.030
So that's something,

00:48:39.030 --> 00:48:41.370
this is a case where they seem to succeed

00:48:41.370 --> 00:48:43.310
in distinguishing their own mental state

00:48:43.310 --> 00:48:45.590
from that of another person.

00:48:45.590 --> 00:48:47.840
Contrast that with this situation.

00:48:47.840 --> 00:48:49.373
We've got the same guy,

00:48:50.360 --> 00:48:52.250
we've got the same pictures,

00:48:52.250 --> 00:48:55.750
but now instead of a picture
being either visible or hidden,

00:48:55.750 --> 00:48:59.420
a picture is either
upright or upside down.

00:48:59.420 --> 00:49:01.037
And one of the two pictures is upright

00:49:01.037 --> 00:49:05.180
and the other picture is
upside down to the actor.

00:49:05.180 --> 00:49:07.600
And the actor's action
is to take the picture

00:49:07.600 --> 00:49:09.800
that's upside down to him and turn it

00:49:09.800 --> 00:49:12.860
so that it's right side up to him, okay?

00:49:12.860 --> 00:49:16.750
And the question to the baby
is, what's this person doing?

00:49:16.750 --> 00:49:20.170
Is he taking a picture
that he sees as upside down

00:49:20.170 --> 00:49:22.240
and making it right side
up as I just described,

00:49:22.240 --> 00:49:24.300
or is he taking a picture
that's right side up,

00:49:24.300 --> 00:49:25.600
namely what the baby sees,

00:49:25.600 --> 00:49:27.360
and turning it so that it's upside down?

00:49:27.360 --> 00:49:29.682
And in this situation,

00:49:29.682 --> 00:49:32.880
they do the egocentric thing
and represent, you know,

00:49:32.880 --> 00:49:34.480
the evidence is that

00:49:34.480 --> 00:49:37.560
they generalized to this display
and look longer at this one

00:49:37.560 --> 00:49:41.070
as if they represented him
as taking an upright picture

00:49:41.070 --> 00:49:43.090
and turning it upside down,

00:49:43.090 --> 00:49:45.050
what they saw from their perspective,

00:49:45.050 --> 00:49:48.320
not the agents' perspective.

00:49:48.320 --> 00:49:50.700
So as old as 15 months,

00:49:50.700 --> 00:49:55.480
infants don't seem to
infer that agents actions

00:49:55.480 --> 00:49:58.670
reflect their experiences of the objects

00:49:58.670 --> 00:50:00.830
that they're acting on.

00:50:00.830 --> 00:50:03.540
They depend on the
visibility of the objects

00:50:03.540 --> 00:50:04.740
that they're acting on,

00:50:04.740 --> 00:50:06.930
but not on the ways in
which they themselves

00:50:06.930 --> 00:50:10.040
are construing that object as
right side up or upside down.

00:50:10.040 --> 00:50:11.910
I'm not gonna show you any more data,

00:50:11.910 --> 00:50:15.090
but we've done this
experiment multiple times

00:50:15.090 --> 00:50:17.780
using different variations on this method,

00:50:17.780 --> 00:50:19.290
and that's consistently been

00:50:20.570 --> 00:50:21.720
the finding that we get.

00:50:21.720 --> 00:50:24.100
We don't yet know why this is.

00:50:24.100 --> 00:50:25.800
We don't know whether this is a...

00:50:25.800 --> 00:50:28.100
There's one thing that's
different about this case

00:50:28.100 --> 00:50:29.220
from the gold case,

00:50:29.220 --> 00:50:32.150
the location versus object
case I described before,

00:50:32.150 --> 00:50:34.650
which is it's not the case,

00:50:34.650 --> 00:50:36.800
it seems like it's not the case that

00:50:36.800 --> 00:50:39.150
they understand this guy's got experiences

00:50:39.150 --> 00:50:41.100
and that experiences can differ,

00:50:41.100 --> 00:50:44.040
but they don't know what his
specific experiences are.

00:50:44.040 --> 00:50:45.500
If that's all that was going on,

00:50:45.500 --> 00:50:47.410
then they should be equal
between these two events

00:50:47.410 --> 00:50:49.350
as they were between the
place and the location.

00:50:49.350 --> 00:50:51.470
And that's not what we see here,

00:50:51.470 --> 00:50:53.540
but we don't know what is going on.

00:50:53.540 --> 00:50:55.880
One possibility is that
kids are just locked

00:50:55.880 --> 00:50:58.390
into their own view of the world.

00:50:58.390 --> 00:51:00.630
I think we have evidence against that, but

00:51:02.410 --> 00:51:03.460
I won't talk about it now

00:51:03.460 --> 00:51:05.670
since you're looking at
your watch, which tells me,

00:51:05.670 --> 00:51:07.340
oh, no, that's terrible,

00:51:07.340 --> 00:51:09.230
and I haven't gotten to
core social cognition yet.

00:51:09.230 --> 00:51:11.300
Okay, I have to go faster.

00:51:11.300 --> 00:51:13.143
All right, nevermind.

00:51:14.558 --> 00:51:16.340
There's at least two possibilities left

00:51:16.340 --> 00:51:18.240
once we rule that first one out.

00:51:18.240 --> 00:51:20.340
One is that they don't have a sense of

00:51:20.340 --> 00:51:22.890
other people's experiences
at all, only of their own.

00:51:22.890 --> 00:51:24.570
I think that's not gonna
turn out to be true,

00:51:24.570 --> 00:51:26.940
but it's possible given these data.

00:51:26.940 --> 00:51:30.810
The other possibility is
that they understand that

00:51:30.810 --> 00:51:32.930
experiences can be variable,

00:51:32.930 --> 00:51:36.910
but they do not yet have
the means for determining

00:51:36.910 --> 00:51:39.140
how they're going to vary for other people

00:51:39.140 --> 00:51:41.400
relative to how they
will vary for themselves.

00:51:41.400 --> 00:51:43.390
And in the absence of that information,

00:51:43.390 --> 00:51:45.700
they fall back on a regularity

00:51:45.700 --> 00:51:48.280
that's actually useful
in many circumstances,

00:51:48.280 --> 00:51:51.070
which is that very often,

00:51:51.070 --> 00:51:53.520
different parties observing the same event

00:51:53.520 --> 00:51:55.240
will have the same experience.

00:51:55.240 --> 00:51:58.100
We're in a sad movie
together and I'll feel sad

00:51:58.100 --> 00:51:59.690
and you'll feel sad as well.

00:51:59.690 --> 00:52:04.690
And infants may be relying
on that as a strategy

00:52:05.030 --> 00:52:06.710
while waiting as maybe

00:52:06.710 --> 00:52:08.470
the pre reaching infants were waiting

00:52:08.470 --> 00:52:10.710
for more information to disambiguate

00:52:10.710 --> 00:52:13.310
what the experiences should be.

00:52:13.310 --> 00:52:14.730
But that's as far as I can go with that.

00:52:14.730 --> 00:52:18.870
Now, I wanna turn to the
studies of social beings,

00:52:18.870 --> 00:52:21.090
and I'll try to get
through this super quickly

00:52:21.090 --> 00:52:23.623
so we get to how children learn.

00:52:25.310 --> 00:52:28.410
Okay, three findings
very quickly on one slide

00:52:28.410 --> 00:52:30.360
suggesting that there's a distinct system

00:52:30.360 --> 00:52:32.250
for representing social beings.

00:52:32.250 --> 00:52:35.680
Infants are highly sensitive to faces

00:52:35.680 --> 00:52:37.560
that are looking at them,

00:52:37.560 --> 00:52:40.990
when peoples change
their facial expressions

00:52:40.990 --> 00:52:44.210
and their facial movements,
they tend to imitate them,

00:52:44.210 --> 00:52:45.780
when people are looking straight ahead

00:52:45.780 --> 00:52:48.750
and then turn to the side
to look to an object,

00:52:48.750 --> 00:52:52.920
they will shift their
attention to the same side

00:52:52.920 --> 00:52:56.059
that the other person has
shifted their gaze to.

00:52:56.059 --> 00:53:00.240
These abilities are all
shown by non-human primates,

00:53:00.240 --> 00:53:03.260
as well as by human infants,

00:53:03.260 --> 00:53:05.450
suggesting that this is an ancient system.

00:53:05.450 --> 00:53:08.370
And I think these
findings also suggest that

00:53:08.370 --> 00:53:12.650
the system centers on
interconnected abstract concepts

00:53:12.650 --> 00:53:17.650
of social engagement and
shareable experiences

00:53:18.420 --> 00:53:21.450
that occur in states of engagement.

00:53:21.450 --> 00:53:23.543
But core social knowledge is limited.

00:53:24.800 --> 00:53:29.750
Infants up until about 10
months, nine or 10 months of age,

00:53:29.750 --> 00:53:33.080
like people who imitate
their social gestures

00:53:33.080 --> 00:53:34.390
and their emotional expressions,

00:53:34.390 --> 00:53:36.130
and they'll tend to smile at them,

00:53:36.130 --> 00:53:37.990
but they're completely unmoved

00:53:37.990 --> 00:53:41.163
by people who imitate
their actions on objects.

00:53:42.840 --> 00:53:47.840
Infants also tend to view when
other people imitate them,

00:53:48.690 --> 00:53:51.810
they view that imitation as social

00:53:51.810 --> 00:53:55.010
if it involves purposeless
actions like waving your hands

00:53:55.010 --> 00:53:56.350
or jumping up and down,

00:53:56.350 --> 00:54:00.310
but not if it involves
instrumental actions on objects,

00:54:00.310 --> 00:54:03.100
suggesting that infants
can represent a person,

00:54:03.100 --> 00:54:05.570
either as an agent or as a social being,

00:54:05.570 --> 00:54:07.490
but not as both at the same time.

00:54:07.490 --> 00:54:10.880
They can't represent one
action as simultaneously social

00:54:10.880 --> 00:54:12.220
and object directed.

00:54:12.220 --> 00:54:16.140
And that may be why until
about 10 months of age,

00:54:16.140 --> 00:54:20.530
infants don't seem to have
any notions of actions

00:54:20.530 --> 00:54:22.240
that are both social and instrumental,

00:54:22.240 --> 00:54:27.240
like gift giving or
competition over resources or

00:54:30.640 --> 00:54:33.300
cooperation or helping.

00:54:33.300 --> 00:54:35.560
So it's an ancient limited system,

00:54:35.560 --> 00:54:37.340
I think, distinct from ancient system.

00:54:37.340 --> 00:54:38.390
And that raises the question,

00:54:38.390 --> 00:54:39.920
does it support infants learning?

00:54:39.920 --> 00:54:43.170
And this is the last experiment
I will tell you about.

00:54:43.170 --> 00:54:46.013
Well, actually, I guess I have
a slide that leads up to it.

00:54:47.641 --> 00:54:51.440
There've been lots of
studies that have shown that

00:54:51.440 --> 00:54:53.480
when you present babies with characters

00:54:53.480 --> 00:54:54.320
that they don't know,

00:54:54.320 --> 00:54:56.120
so they're just third party observers

00:54:56.120 --> 00:54:58.660
to social interactions
among other characters,

00:54:58.660 --> 00:55:01.350
they expect characters
who imitate one another

00:55:01.350 --> 00:55:03.390
to affiliate with each other,

00:55:03.390 --> 00:55:06.900
they expect characters who comfort younger

00:55:07.790 --> 00:55:10.750
smaller characters making
crying baby noises.

00:55:10.750 --> 00:55:14.320
If two adult characters seem
to comfort the same baby,

00:55:14.320 --> 00:55:17.400
they expect them to
affiliate with each other.

00:55:17.400 --> 00:55:20.320
But what wasn't revealed
by any of these studies is

00:55:20.320 --> 00:55:22.090
what's the function of these abilities?

00:55:22.090 --> 00:55:24.420
And might they guide infants learning

00:55:24.420 --> 00:55:26.970
about their own social world?

00:55:26.970 --> 00:55:29.820
And that's the question
that Ashley Thomas,

00:55:29.820 --> 00:55:32.610
who also gets all the credit
for the saliva sharing studies

00:55:32.610 --> 00:55:34.270
that I won't talk about

00:55:34.270 --> 00:55:37.890
has been exploring with
Rebecca Saxe and me recently.

00:55:37.890 --> 00:55:41.950
Here are some studies over
Zoom that she has conducted.

00:55:41.950 --> 00:55:43.430
Now, for these studies,

00:55:43.430 --> 00:55:45.120
she first works with parents

00:55:45.120 --> 00:55:47.970
and gets parents to make
movies of themselves

00:55:47.970 --> 00:55:50.940
using their webcam in which
they're staring straight ahead

00:55:50.940 --> 00:55:52.530
and then making nonsense noises

00:55:52.530 --> 00:55:55.020
and looking to one side or
looking to the other side.

00:55:55.020 --> 00:55:56.560
And then she takes these movies

00:55:56.560 --> 00:56:00.050
and digitally manipulates them
to add puppets to the movies

00:56:00.050 --> 00:56:03.320
so that she can present
infants with a movie

00:56:03.320 --> 00:56:05.420
on their laptop screen at home

00:56:05.420 --> 00:56:08.330
in which their parent turns to a puppet.

00:56:08.330 --> 00:56:10.160
The puppet says something to the parent

00:56:10.160 --> 00:56:11.640
and the parent imitates the puppet

00:56:11.640 --> 00:56:13.390
by repeating what they said.

00:56:13.390 --> 00:56:16.290
So the structure of the
study is there's two puppets,

00:56:16.290 --> 00:56:18.100
parent imitates one puppet,

00:56:18.100 --> 00:56:20.610
does not imitate the other puppet,

00:56:20.610 --> 00:56:22.320
and then the parent goes away.

00:56:22.320 --> 00:56:23.670
The two puppets are left,

00:56:23.670 --> 00:56:25.840
they move their mouths in synchrony,

00:56:25.840 --> 00:56:29.420
and the baby hears,
hi, Brian, how are you?

00:56:29.420 --> 00:56:31.900
And the question is Brian
being the name of the baby.

00:56:31.900 --> 00:56:33.020
The question is,

00:56:33.020 --> 00:56:36.410
who does the baby think knows their name?

00:56:36.410 --> 00:56:40.620
Is it the person that
their parent imitated?

00:56:40.620 --> 00:56:42.800
So the full design of this study is

00:56:42.800 --> 00:56:45.320
every baby has run
through this study twice

00:56:45.320 --> 00:56:46.670
with two different adults

00:56:46.670 --> 00:56:48.780
and two different sets of puppets.

00:56:48.780 --> 00:56:50.370
One of the adults is their parent,

00:56:50.370 --> 00:56:54.163
the other adult is the parent
of another baby in the study.

00:56:55.270 --> 00:56:57.080
They get to see the name calling

00:56:57.080 --> 00:56:58.790
and they also get to see control events

00:56:58.790 --> 00:57:00.530
where puppets dance to music.

00:57:00.530 --> 00:57:03.520
There was never any
differential looking at the,

00:57:03.520 --> 00:57:05.900
during the control event,
so I won't show you that.

00:57:05.900 --> 00:57:06.740
I'll just show you

00:57:06.740 --> 00:57:09.280
they're looking to the
puppet who called their name.

00:57:09.280 --> 00:57:10.530
And what you see is that

00:57:10.530 --> 00:57:13.680
when that puppet had been
imitated by their own parent,

00:57:13.680 --> 00:57:15.270
they looked to that puppet

00:57:15.270 --> 00:57:16.930
when they heard their name being called.

00:57:16.930 --> 00:57:18.190
But when they saw a puppet

00:57:18.190 --> 00:57:21.710
who had been imitated by
another parent, they did not.

00:57:21.710 --> 00:57:23.170
So it looks like

00:57:25.659 --> 00:57:29.110
they're making an inference
about who's connected to them

00:57:29.110 --> 00:57:34.110
from information about who
is connected to their parent.

00:57:34.800 --> 00:57:36.900
But what the study didn't yet tell us is

00:57:36.900 --> 00:57:38.530
what kind of inference are they making?

00:57:38.530 --> 00:57:41.980
Is it about a trait or is
it about a relationship?

00:57:41.980 --> 00:57:44.860
This person is part of my social network.

00:57:44.860 --> 00:57:46.960
So to get at that,
Ashley's done more studies.

00:57:46.960 --> 00:57:49.420
I'll tell you just one
of them, where again,

00:57:49.420 --> 00:57:52.190
you have a parent condition
and a stranger condition,

00:57:52.190 --> 00:57:55.230
but in addition to having conditions

00:57:55.230 --> 00:57:58.010
in which they see puppets who,

00:57:58.010 --> 00:58:00.600
experiments in which they
see puppets who face them

00:58:00.600 --> 00:58:03.760
and call to, and hear a voice
calling to them by name,

00:58:03.760 --> 00:58:06.130
there's a comparison condition
in which they see puppets

00:58:06.130 --> 00:58:08.310
who face away from them and hear a voice

00:58:08.310 --> 00:58:11.760
calling the name of another
baby in the study, right?

00:58:11.760 --> 00:58:13.420
So if they just think these puppets

00:58:13.420 --> 00:58:14.900
are more likely to be friendly,

00:58:14.900 --> 00:58:17.500
you should see the same
effects in the two conditions.

00:58:17.500 --> 00:58:20.140
But if you think it's really about them,

00:58:20.140 --> 00:58:21.290
you'll see different effects.

00:58:21.290 --> 00:58:24.530
And what she in fact gets
looking both at first looks

00:58:24.530 --> 00:58:26.920
and also total looks, oh,
I'm gonna be shut up soon,

00:58:26.920 --> 00:58:28.890
- [Woman] It makes me be the bad puppet.

00:58:28.890 --> 00:58:30.260
- Yeah, okay.

00:58:30.260 --> 00:58:34.640
Is that the only effect is
when they've seen their parent,

00:58:34.640 --> 00:58:38.440
the puppet imitated by their
parent calling to them,

00:58:38.440 --> 00:58:41.620
not calling to anybody else.

00:58:41.620 --> 00:58:43.503
So, final study,

00:58:44.440 --> 00:58:47.699
is this only about the baby,

00:58:47.699 --> 00:58:50.520
is the baby only concerned
who's connected to me

00:58:50.520 --> 00:58:52.360
or is the baby also concerned

00:58:52.360 --> 00:58:54.670
who's connected to the
people that I care about?

00:58:54.670 --> 00:58:57.250
Last study, I'll tell
you about, I promise.

00:58:57.250 --> 00:58:59.030
So to get at that,

00:58:59.030 --> 00:59:00.600
Ashley did one further study

00:59:00.600 --> 00:59:03.160
where she now begins with two puppets,

00:59:03.160 --> 00:59:05.070
one of whom calls the baby by name

00:59:05.070 --> 00:59:08.900
and the other dances in an
equally engaging fashion.

00:59:08.900 --> 00:59:11.040
Babies are equally interested
in these two events,

00:59:11.040 --> 00:59:14.230
but shows no sign of individual
recognition of the baby.

00:59:14.230 --> 00:59:16.990
And then they see events in which

00:59:16.990 --> 00:59:21.590
an adult expresses distress to
the two puppets are present,

00:59:21.590 --> 00:59:22.940
they turn toward the adult.

00:59:22.940 --> 00:59:23.850
And the question is,

00:59:23.850 --> 00:59:24.850
who does the baby think

00:59:24.850 --> 00:59:28.140
is gonna comfort this adult in distress?

00:59:28.140 --> 00:59:31.470
And the baby is run
through two experiments,

00:59:31.470 --> 00:59:33.950
one in which the adult is their own parent

00:59:33.950 --> 00:59:35.080
and the other in which the adult

00:59:35.080 --> 00:59:37.000
is a parent of another baby.

00:59:37.000 --> 00:59:39.770
And as you can see, when
it's their own parent,

00:59:39.770 --> 00:59:43.070
they expect the parent to
be comforted by the puppet

00:59:43.070 --> 00:59:45.620
who knew their name,
not by the other puppet.

00:59:45.620 --> 00:59:47.370
So it looks like they're not only learning

00:59:47.370 --> 00:59:48.410
who's connected to me,

00:59:48.410 --> 00:59:51.130
but what are the contours
of my social world?

00:59:51.130 --> 00:59:54.340
They're starting to learn that in infancy.

00:59:54.340 --> 00:59:57.480
So I think what we have here
are three distinct and limited

00:59:57.480 --> 01:00:00.850
but unitarian abstract
systems of core knowledge,

01:00:00.850 --> 01:00:05.230
a system focused on
objects that move on agents

01:00:05.230 --> 01:00:07.320
that act and on social beings

01:00:07.320 --> 01:00:10.740
that engage with one another
and share experiences.

01:00:10.740 --> 01:00:12.180
I think we also see evidence that

01:00:12.180 --> 01:00:15.360
infants don't readily
combine these systems,

01:00:15.360 --> 01:00:17.920
even though two of the systems at least,

01:00:17.920 --> 01:00:20.970
focus on the very same individuals,

01:00:20.970 --> 01:00:23.440
but when an individual
is acting on an object

01:00:23.440 --> 01:00:24.701
that engages one system

01:00:24.701 --> 01:00:27.720
when they're engaging
with another individual,

01:00:27.720 --> 01:00:30.060
it engages another.

01:00:30.060 --> 01:00:33.170
But children's fast and flexible learning

01:00:33.170 --> 01:00:35.570
can't depend on these systems alone

01:00:35.570 --> 01:00:38.810
because they're all
shared by other animals,

01:00:38.810 --> 01:00:40.950
and other animals don't learn,

01:00:40.950 --> 01:00:43.680
don't undergo this
explosive cultural learning

01:00:43.680 --> 01:00:45.890
that you see in children
after the first year,

01:00:45.890 --> 01:00:47.930
and because core knowledge is limited

01:00:47.930 --> 01:00:51.570
in the ways that I've already described.

01:00:51.570 --> 01:00:54.250
So that then raises the question,

01:00:54.250 --> 01:00:56.820
what accounts for infants
uniquely human learning?

01:00:56.820 --> 01:01:00.610
And clearly, I don't have time
to address that question now.

01:01:00.610 --> 01:01:02.030
I have slides addressing it.

01:01:02.030 --> 01:01:04.250
I did not as,

01:01:04.250 --> 01:01:06.120
this is not the first time
this has happened to me,

01:01:06.120 --> 01:01:08.630
that I've confidently thought
I would be done in 45 minutes

01:01:08.630 --> 01:01:10.110
and I wasn't.

01:01:10.110 --> 01:01:12.580
But let me just say that

01:01:12.580 --> 01:01:14.750
I think we see hints of an answer

01:01:14.750 --> 01:01:16.720
if we look at another developmental change

01:01:16.720 --> 01:01:19.800
that occurs over the first year of life,

01:01:19.800 --> 01:01:22.690
and actually starts before birth.

01:01:22.690 --> 01:01:25.610
And that is that over the
course of the first year,

01:01:25.610 --> 01:01:29.430
children learn a great deal
about the language of the people

01:01:29.430 --> 01:01:30.960
in their social world.

01:01:30.960 --> 01:01:32.220
They learn what it sounds.

01:01:32.220 --> 01:01:36.150
They learn about language at
all levels simultaneously.

01:01:36.150 --> 01:01:37.630
They learn about its phenology.

01:01:37.630 --> 01:01:38.940
They learn about its prosody,

01:01:38.940 --> 01:01:41.780
but they also learn the meanings of words,

01:01:41.780 --> 01:01:43.760
starting with recognizing their own name

01:01:43.760 --> 01:01:45.780
at about four months of age.

01:01:45.780 --> 01:01:47.530
They learn the grammatical distinction

01:01:47.530 --> 01:01:50.090
between function words and content words.

01:01:50.090 --> 01:01:52.500
And they know that they
come to discover that

01:01:52.500 --> 01:01:54.870
content words refer to
things in the world,

01:01:54.870 --> 01:01:57.610
whereas function words pick
out the relationships among,

01:01:57.610 --> 01:02:00.670
refer to relationships among those things.

01:02:00.670 --> 01:02:03.600
All of this they learn
before they start to talk.

01:02:03.600 --> 01:02:06.060
And also before they start to talk,

01:02:06.060 --> 01:02:08.940
by learning these things
about the language

01:02:08.940 --> 01:02:10.310
that people are speaking,

01:02:10.310 --> 01:02:12.320
they learn things about those people,

01:02:12.320 --> 01:02:13.940
and they learn things about the world

01:02:13.940 --> 01:02:16.210
that those people are speaking about.

01:02:16.210 --> 01:02:17.170
About the people,

01:02:17.170 --> 01:02:20.520
I think they learn that
they are social agents

01:02:20.520 --> 01:02:24.280
whose actions can be
simultaneously social and agentive.

01:02:24.280 --> 01:02:27.170
And I think they learn
this when they hear people

01:02:27.170 --> 01:02:29.170
saying things like look at the cup,

01:02:29.170 --> 01:02:32.190
look at the bottle.

01:02:32.190 --> 01:02:35.180
Look is inviting the
child to engage with them

01:02:35.180 --> 01:02:36.600
and sharing experience,

01:02:36.600 --> 01:02:38.250
cup or bottle is inviting them

01:02:38.250 --> 01:02:41.400
to share an experience of an object, okay?

01:02:41.400 --> 01:02:43.740
I think this brings a
new conception of people

01:02:43.740 --> 01:02:45.340
and their mental states,

01:02:45.340 --> 01:02:49.510
but it also gives children
an extremely useful tool

01:02:49.510 --> 01:02:51.360
for learning about the world

01:02:51.360 --> 01:02:54.330
because people are agents

01:02:54.330 --> 01:02:56.650
and therefore speak efficiently,

01:02:56.650 --> 01:02:58.680
and they are also social beings,

01:02:58.680 --> 01:03:00.877
and they therefore speak relevantly

01:03:00.877 --> 01:03:03.400
and informatively and helpfully.

01:03:03.400 --> 01:03:06.320
And whether people are
speaking to the infant

01:03:06.320 --> 01:03:09.650
or to somebody else or to themselves,

01:03:09.650 --> 01:03:10.953
when they talk,

01:03:12.821 --> 01:03:15.970
they make distinctions
in what they call things

01:03:15.970 --> 01:03:17.510
that reflect the distinctions

01:03:17.510 --> 01:03:19.520
that they think are worth conveying

01:03:19.520 --> 01:03:22.820
and relevant in the situation
in which they are in.

01:03:22.820 --> 01:03:26.770
So if in an experiment by Xu and Carey

01:03:26.770 --> 01:03:29.630
where a 10 month old baby would normally

01:03:30.857 --> 01:03:33.120
not be able to tell you whether
there's a cup, you know,

01:03:33.120 --> 01:03:38.120
a shoe on one side and a duck
on the other side of a screen,

01:03:38.180 --> 01:03:40.660
when they hear somebody calling to them

01:03:40.660 --> 01:03:44.170
and labeling those objects
with different words,

01:03:44.170 --> 01:03:47.360
they'll infer that there's
two different objects there,

01:03:47.360 --> 01:03:49.070
even if they've never seen anything.

01:03:49.070 --> 01:03:50.930
Persons just looked into a box and said,

01:03:50.930 --> 01:03:52.950
look, a blickit, atoma.

01:03:52.950 --> 01:03:55.210
They'll infer that there
are two objects in that box

01:03:55.210 --> 01:03:58.360
because you wouldn't go to
the effort of using two words

01:03:58.360 --> 01:04:01.620
if you were only
describing one thing twice.

01:04:01.620 --> 01:04:03.100
So language becomes

01:04:03.100 --> 01:04:06.400
an incredibly useful source
of information for children

01:04:06.400 --> 01:04:08.990
to discover what the
cultural distinctions are

01:04:08.990 --> 01:04:10.520
in the world that they're living in.

01:04:10.520 --> 01:04:12.650
And I think this is the beginning hint

01:04:12.650 --> 01:04:14.340
to how children come to learn

01:04:15.670 --> 01:04:17.890
so fast and flexibly

01:04:17.890 --> 01:04:20.160
about the particular
environments they're in,

01:04:20.160 --> 01:04:22.690
even though the core knowledge
that they're equipped with

01:04:22.690 --> 01:04:24.420
is utterly uninformative

01:04:24.420 --> 01:04:27.040
about the differences between
one environment and another,

01:04:27.040 --> 01:04:29.910
since it only centers on
these abstract concepts

01:04:29.910 --> 01:04:34.630
that apply in every environment
in exactly the same way.

01:04:34.630 --> 01:04:38.090
And I think with this learning system,

01:04:38.090 --> 01:04:40.630
we get engaged in a process that leads to

01:04:40.630 --> 01:04:43.350
the situation we find
ourselves in as adults

01:04:43.350 --> 01:04:46.730
where the vast majority of
the things that we know,

01:04:46.730 --> 01:04:48.970
we do not know from our direct experience.

01:04:48.970 --> 01:04:51.520
We know from other people.

01:04:51.520 --> 01:04:53.280
Either people we know directly

01:04:53.280 --> 01:04:57.440
or people who reach us
through news services

01:04:57.440 --> 01:04:59.800
or social media or other things.

01:04:59.800 --> 01:05:04.310
I think this is both our
greatest gift for learning

01:05:04.310 --> 01:05:09.310
and also an extreme potential
source of vulnerability,

01:05:09.800 --> 01:05:11.700
because we're going to learn most readily

01:05:11.700 --> 01:05:13.200
from sources that we trust,

01:05:13.200 --> 01:05:15.290
and if we don't all
trust the same sources,

01:05:15.290 --> 01:05:16.950
that can bring us lots of problems.

01:05:16.950 --> 01:05:19.180
And then the parting hope of this talk

01:05:19.180 --> 01:05:22.900
had I been able to give
the last third of it was-

01:05:22.900 --> 01:05:23.950
- [Woman] We're going for an hour now.

01:05:23.950 --> 01:05:26.130
- Okay, I'm done, I'm done.

01:05:26.130 --> 01:05:28.760
I think maybe if we understand
all this stuff better,

01:05:28.760 --> 01:05:30.580
we can start to deal with
some of those problems.

01:05:30.580 --> 01:05:31.959
Thank you.
- [Woman] Thank you.

01:05:31.959 --> 01:05:34.709
(all applauding)

01:05:37.360 --> 01:05:38.193
- Oh.

01:05:39.910 --> 01:05:40.743
- [Woman] Yeah.

01:05:41.690 --> 01:05:44.090
- [Woman] We have time
for one or two questions.

01:05:55.600 --> 01:05:58.280
- Hi, I have a question about

01:05:58.280 --> 01:06:02.590
running the studies with
the babies over Zoom.

01:06:02.590 --> 01:06:05.360
I actually used to work
in Karen Winn's lab

01:06:05.360 --> 01:06:06.193
before it shut down.

01:06:06.193 --> 01:06:07.923
So that's pretty interesting to me.

01:06:09.290 --> 01:06:12.280
But I remember learning
in one of my classes that

01:06:13.170 --> 01:06:15.260
some things can't be learned on a screen,

01:06:15.260 --> 01:06:17.260
like a baby can only
learn to differentiate

01:06:17.260 --> 01:06:19.110
the phonemes of a non-native language,

01:06:19.110 --> 01:06:22.740
like from a speaker,
not from watching a TV

01:06:22.740 --> 01:06:23.690
or watching a movie

01:06:24.710 --> 01:06:25.790
or someone on the screen.

01:06:25.790 --> 01:06:27.230
So I'm wondering if there,

01:06:27.230 --> 01:06:29.200
are there other limitations
that you've encountered

01:06:29.200 --> 01:06:32.030
that cannot be learned through the screen?

01:06:32.030 --> 01:06:33.330
- That's a great question.

01:06:34.700 --> 01:06:37.450
The condition, the study
that you're talking about

01:06:37.450 --> 01:06:39.003
is actually really interesting.

01:06:40.150 --> 01:06:42.400
It's a language learning study,

01:06:42.400 --> 01:06:45.190
and kids are exposed to a Chinese speaker,

01:06:45.190 --> 01:06:46.610
and they either have the Chinese speaker

01:06:46.610 --> 01:06:49.690
come into their house and
talk to them directly,

01:06:49.690 --> 01:06:51.910
or they see a video of the Chinese speaker

01:06:51.910 --> 01:06:54.460
who's gone into somebody
else's house and talked to

01:06:55.929 --> 01:06:56.770
the people there.

01:06:56.770 --> 01:07:00.400
And there's no question that
the non-interactive video

01:07:00.400 --> 01:07:01.690
led to no learning.

01:07:01.690 --> 01:07:03.150
Just like children don't learn language

01:07:03.150 --> 01:07:04.600
from the radio,

01:07:04.600 --> 01:07:07.510
they don't learn language
under that situation.

01:07:07.510 --> 01:07:10.990
What we do over Zoom though,
is interactive video.

01:07:10.990 --> 01:07:14.190
So there's a real experimenter
there who talks to the baby

01:07:14.190 --> 01:07:15.660
at the beginning of the study

01:07:15.660 --> 01:07:18.120
that mom is involved
in setting up the study

01:07:18.120 --> 01:07:21.340
and mom is conversing with
the experimenter at that time.

01:07:21.340 --> 01:07:23.540
The baby gets to see all of that.

01:07:23.540 --> 01:07:26.220
These babies also were being
tested during the pandemic,

01:07:26.220 --> 01:07:29.200
which means they're interacting
with grandparents over video

01:07:29.200 --> 01:07:31.920
and other family relatives.

01:07:31.920 --> 01:07:34.893
It was really the ideal
time to run a study.

01:07:35.900 --> 01:07:37.290
But I have to tell you,

01:07:37.290 --> 01:07:40.790
I was very pessimistic when
we started working with Zoom.

01:07:40.790 --> 01:07:43.860
The reason we started working
with Zoom is that there were

01:07:43.860 --> 01:07:46.880
grad students in my lab who
were highly optimistic about it.

01:07:46.880 --> 01:07:50.077
And they talked me out of
my pessimism and, you know,

01:07:50.077 --> 01:07:51.600
and so we tried it out.

01:07:51.600 --> 01:07:52.930
But I thought there were a hundred reasons

01:07:52.930 --> 01:07:54.160
why these studies wouldn't work,

01:07:54.160 --> 01:07:56.010
and you've mentioned one of them.

01:07:56.010 --> 01:07:58.110
Another reason why I thought
they wouldn't work is that

01:07:58.110 --> 01:08:00.130
the environments would be so variable

01:08:00.130 --> 01:08:02.010
and full of distractions.

01:08:02.010 --> 01:08:03.630
We're testing kids in their kitchens,

01:08:03.630 --> 01:08:06.450
there's dogs running around,
the phone is ringing.

01:08:06.450 --> 01:08:08.080
I mean, you know, all kinds of,

01:08:08.080 --> 01:08:09.510
and we're measuring looking time.

01:08:09.510 --> 01:08:11.160
I thought this will never work.

01:08:11.160 --> 01:08:13.310
It works so well.

01:08:13.310 --> 01:08:17.200
The babies are so much more
relaxed in their own kitchen

01:08:17.200 --> 01:08:19.490
than they ever were when
they came into my lab,

01:08:19.490 --> 01:08:21.530
and we went into the
sound attenuated rooms

01:08:21.530 --> 01:08:23.120
and we turned the lights down

01:08:23.120 --> 01:08:24.300
and they would look around like,

01:08:24.300 --> 01:08:26.730
what kind of weird environment is this?

01:08:26.730 --> 01:08:29.400
And the parents would sometimes
look a little ill at ease.

01:08:29.400 --> 01:08:31.270
At home, everybody is relaxed.

01:08:31.270 --> 01:08:33.970
The kids are watching the
videos, it works super well.

01:08:33.970 --> 01:08:36.200
And we haven't yet found a situation

01:08:37.360 --> 01:08:39.400
where we can run the tests at all.

01:08:39.400 --> 01:08:42.050
That hasn't worked as well on video

01:08:42.050 --> 01:08:45.140
as it used to work in the lab, generally.

01:08:45.140 --> 01:08:47.860
For most of our studies,
we never wanna go back.

01:08:47.860 --> 01:08:50.070
However, there are things we can't do.

01:08:50.070 --> 01:08:52.810
And one of them actually was a
study that was maybe going on

01:08:52.810 --> 01:08:55.900
in Karen's lab when you were still there,

01:08:55.900 --> 01:08:59.150
looking at whether
presenting kids with plants.

01:08:59.150 --> 01:09:01.040
I really wanted to do these studies,

01:09:01.040 --> 01:09:03.150
present kids with plants and see

01:09:03.150 --> 01:09:05.690
whether they would
distinguish plants from art,

01:09:05.690 --> 01:09:09.320
you know, living plants
from inanimate artifacts

01:09:09.320 --> 01:09:11.290
by their forms and functions.

01:09:11.290 --> 01:09:15.580
And for that, the outcome
measure that Karen used was

01:09:15.580 --> 01:09:17.310
a latency to touch an object.

01:09:17.310 --> 01:09:19.100
Would they be more
hesitant to touch a plant

01:09:19.100 --> 01:09:20.810
than to touch an artifact?

01:09:20.810 --> 01:09:22.540
And that, I don't know
how to do over Zoom.

01:09:22.540 --> 01:09:25.310
So we haven't been able to run everything,

01:09:25.310 --> 01:09:26.930
but I think you're absolutely right that

01:09:26.930 --> 01:09:28.980
kids are predisposed to learn from people

01:09:29.870 --> 01:09:31.550
that they feel socially connected to.

01:09:31.550 --> 01:09:34.050
It's just that in the pandemic era,

01:09:34.050 --> 01:09:36.693
you can achieve that
with interactive video.

01:09:40.820 --> 01:09:41.653
Yeah.

01:09:50.680 --> 01:09:54.013
- Hi, thanks for the
really wonderful talk.

01:09:55.630 --> 01:09:58.210
I just wanted to ask a question about

01:09:58.210 --> 01:10:02.300
the neonatal kind of
imitation that was featured

01:10:02.300 --> 01:10:04.400
several times in your talk.

01:10:04.400 --> 01:10:06.460
There's some like recent
work that suggests that

01:10:06.460 --> 01:10:09.620
there are alternatives
to the imitation theory

01:10:09.620 --> 01:10:12.130
for the neonatal work.

01:10:12.130 --> 01:10:13.740
Like one of them being that like

01:10:14.830 --> 01:10:16.890
for very young babies and neonates,

01:10:16.890 --> 01:10:19.570
that they tend to make
stereotypic movements.

01:10:19.570 --> 01:10:21.460
And when they see a
human and they're excited

01:10:21.460 --> 01:10:23.570
and they're more likely
to make those gestures.

01:10:23.570 --> 01:10:26.780
So, you know, those work,

01:10:26.780 --> 01:10:29.160
they haven't been reliably replicated.

01:10:29.160 --> 01:10:30.240
And like, theoretically,

01:10:30.240 --> 01:10:33.460
there has been some lots
of interesting papers

01:10:33.460 --> 01:10:36.400
that discuss these alternatives.

01:10:36.400 --> 01:10:38.710
I mean, I don't think from the
core knowledge perspective,

01:10:38.710 --> 01:10:40.890
like it doesn't make a,

01:10:40.890 --> 01:10:42.810
my perspective, it
doesn't make a huge impact

01:10:42.810 --> 01:10:46.720
to the extent that you see
this in other primates as well.

01:10:46.720 --> 01:10:48.380
But I just wonder if,

01:10:48.380 --> 01:10:50.290
and of course, argumentation

01:10:50.290 --> 01:10:52.183
plays a huge role in development.

01:10:52.183 --> 01:10:54.820
I just think maybe a little bit later.

01:10:54.820 --> 01:10:57.090
- Yeah, so you're,

01:10:57.090 --> 01:10:59.460
are you talking mostly about
the current biology paper

01:10:59.460 --> 01:11:00.540
that came out like four,

01:11:00.540 --> 01:11:01.850
three years ago?
- Yes, hays and

01:11:01.850 --> 01:11:03.955
- Yeah.
- there's a also-

01:11:03.955 --> 01:11:05.630
- I don't read that as a non replication

01:11:05.630 --> 01:11:08.810
of the imitation studies.

01:11:08.810 --> 01:11:11.760
What the original imitation
studies had shown,

01:11:11.760 --> 01:11:14.540
this by the way, is all
work by Andy Meltzoff,

01:11:14.540 --> 01:11:17.250
who has a nice reply to
that current biology paper

01:11:17.250 --> 01:11:18.470
in developmental science.

01:11:18.470 --> 01:11:23.470
But what the original imitation
studies had shown is that

01:11:23.690 --> 01:11:27.110
not that infants are
motorically capable of

01:11:27.110 --> 01:11:30.240
precisely imitating what
someone else does, not at all,

01:11:30.240 --> 01:11:32.293
but that if you give them enough time,

01:11:33.260 --> 01:11:37.170
they will attempt to
reproduce as best they can

01:11:37.170 --> 01:11:39.390
the movements that they saw.

01:11:39.390 --> 01:11:41.980
And they will not only
do that immediately,

01:11:41.980 --> 01:11:43.920
they'll do it after a delay of an hour,

01:11:43.920 --> 01:11:46.300
and they'll do it after a delay of a day

01:11:46.300 --> 01:11:48.480
if they see that same person again.

01:11:48.480 --> 01:11:50.950
Now, what was done in
the current biology paper

01:11:50.950 --> 01:11:54.630
is they saw, the babies
got to see a single person,

01:11:54.630 --> 01:11:56.600
they did one gesture,

01:11:56.600 --> 01:11:59.330
they gave the baby, I
think, a minute to respond,

01:11:59.330 --> 01:12:01.450
maybe 30 seconds, not very long.

01:12:01.450 --> 01:12:03.050
Then they did a second gesture,

01:12:03.050 --> 01:12:04.270
then they did a third gesture,

01:12:04.270 --> 01:12:05.150
then they did a fourth.

01:12:05.150 --> 01:12:07.430
Different gestures each time.

01:12:07.430 --> 01:12:08.660
That had never been done

01:12:08.660 --> 01:12:10.800
in the original imitation literature.

01:12:10.800 --> 01:12:14.840
What they reported is that
babies imitated all the gestures,

01:12:14.840 --> 01:12:17.940
but they didn't imitate it
in that first 32nd window.

01:12:17.940 --> 01:12:21.380
It would take them longer to
produce the gestures over time.

01:12:21.380 --> 01:12:24.100
And what Andy Meltzoff has argued is

01:12:24.100 --> 01:12:26.900
it was because they used that method

01:12:26.900 --> 01:12:28.920
that what they found at the end was, yes,

01:12:28.920 --> 01:12:30.190
we see all these gestures,

01:12:30.190 --> 01:12:32.790
but they're not immediately
following what they saw before.

01:12:32.790 --> 01:12:35.940
And Andy agrees, it doesn't
occur immediately after.

01:12:35.940 --> 01:12:39.360
But he argues that it does occur,

01:12:39.360 --> 01:12:41.420
and the paper where he argues this

01:12:41.420 --> 01:12:43.390
draws both on the data from human infants

01:12:43.390 --> 01:12:45.800
and also on data from studies

01:12:45.800 --> 01:12:47.690
that he wasn't even involved in

01:12:47.690 --> 01:12:50.080
that were conducted with
monkeys and with chimpanzees

01:12:50.080 --> 01:12:51.450
that showed the same effects.

01:12:51.450 --> 01:12:55.710
This very slow, delayed,
partial reproduction.

01:12:55.710 --> 01:12:59.300
By the way, what I think is
interesting about imitation,

01:12:59.300 --> 01:13:01.130
for years, I was puzzled by imitation.

01:13:01.130 --> 01:13:03.160
Why are babies doing this?

01:13:03.160 --> 01:13:05.840
I think the reason that
they're doing it is

01:13:05.840 --> 01:13:08.850
it's the one reliable signal

01:13:08.850 --> 01:13:10.790
that they're able to give to someone

01:13:10.790 --> 01:13:12.940
that they're attending to that person,

01:13:12.940 --> 01:13:15.330
and they're motivated to engage with them

01:13:15.330 --> 01:13:16.790
in the moment, right?

01:13:16.790 --> 01:13:19.500
They can't use language to say that.

01:13:19.500 --> 01:13:21.630
They don't have any
specific cultural knowledge

01:13:21.630 --> 01:13:24.430
that they can draw on to
indicate that to somebody.

01:13:24.430 --> 01:13:29.200
The one thing they can do
is copy somebody's action

01:13:29.200 --> 01:13:31.450
and it's something that's very unlikely

01:13:31.450 --> 01:13:33.560
to happen simply randomly by chance

01:13:33.560 --> 01:13:36.400
if you weren't both
attending to that person

01:13:36.400 --> 01:13:38.860
and motivated to signal

01:13:38.860 --> 01:13:40.530
your state of attention and engagement.

01:13:40.530 --> 01:13:44.550
So that's all, I think that
the imitation is about.

01:13:44.550 --> 01:13:48.220
It's been eliciting
controversies though for

01:13:49.320 --> 01:13:51.320
40 years now

01:13:51.320 --> 01:13:54.650
since the original
reports in the seventies.

01:13:54.650 --> 01:13:57.370
And it's clear that these
are very subtle phenomena

01:13:57.370 --> 01:14:00.580
that you see early on.

01:14:00.580 --> 01:14:03.700
I wouldn't put a lot
of stock on this being

01:14:03.700 --> 01:14:05.130
full-blown imitation,

01:14:05.130 --> 01:14:07.960
but I do think it's saying
something about a baby's effort

01:14:07.960 --> 01:14:10.870
to signal to somebody that
they're engaged with them,

01:14:10.870 --> 01:14:13.600
maybe in the same way that
these kind of movements

01:14:13.600 --> 01:14:16.600
that a neonate will engage in
might be an effort to signal

01:14:16.600 --> 01:14:18.360
I'd really like to be able
to reach for that thing

01:14:18.360 --> 01:14:20.260
even though I can't do that right now.

01:14:21.790 --> 01:14:23.643
- [Woman] One last question from Joanne.

01:14:26.800 --> 01:14:30.540
- I'm interested to hear you
say a little bit more about

01:14:32.230 --> 01:14:35.310
the special role that language plays

01:14:35.310 --> 01:14:38.200
in sort of human cognition

01:14:38.200 --> 01:14:41.220
'cause we've talked about all
these different core systems

01:14:41.220 --> 01:14:42.950
and they're sort of separate

01:14:42.950 --> 01:14:44.540
and we can't use them at the same time,

01:14:44.540 --> 01:14:46.220
but then you were saying
something really interesting

01:14:46.220 --> 01:14:47.053
right at the end

01:14:47.053 --> 01:14:48.962
and I wanna give you a chance
to say a little bit more

01:14:48.962 --> 01:14:51.540
about this.
- Thank you.

01:14:51.540 --> 01:14:54.620
- About how language is an interesting

01:14:54.620 --> 01:14:57.230
sort of integration of
some of these systems.

01:14:57.230 --> 01:14:59.150
And it seems like really plausible for me

01:14:59.150 --> 01:15:00.400
when it comes to like

01:15:01.630 --> 01:15:06.600
maybe object and social stuff.

01:15:06.600 --> 01:15:08.380
But I was wondering if, yeah,

01:15:08.380 --> 01:15:10.840
if other systems get integrated

01:15:10.840 --> 01:15:13.540
by this sort of language process as well,

01:15:13.540 --> 01:15:14.490
or, yeah.
- Yeah,

01:15:14.490 --> 01:15:16.020
that's actually what my whole second book

01:15:16.020 --> 01:15:17.370
is gonna be about,

01:15:17.370 --> 01:15:20.740
because I think there's
many, many cases, not only

01:15:22.810 --> 01:15:25.410
infants developing
understanding of other people

01:15:25.410 --> 01:15:28.260
or infants developing
ability to categorize objects

01:15:28.260 --> 01:15:29.900
as instances of kinds,

01:15:29.900 --> 01:15:31.810
which are the two things
that I think we see

01:15:31.810 --> 01:15:34.760
infants using language for
at the end of the first year,

01:15:34.760 --> 01:15:36.800
but also other abilities like

01:15:37.800 --> 01:15:40.810
their discovery of the natural numbers,

01:15:40.810 --> 01:15:43.770
their discovery of other symbol systems

01:15:43.770 --> 01:15:46.560
and developing abilities to
communicate with other people

01:15:46.560 --> 01:15:49.310
by means of spatial symbols, for example,

01:15:49.310 --> 01:15:52.260
of the sort that will
eventually figure in maps,

01:15:52.260 --> 01:15:55.823
and reading, and pictures,
and things like that.

01:15:56.670 --> 01:15:59.120
So, yeah, I think there's, you know,

01:15:59.120 --> 01:16:00.533
there are many domains,

01:16:01.370 --> 01:16:03.030
as far as I can see, actually,

01:16:03.030 --> 01:16:05.780
in every domain where you
see children's knowledge

01:16:05.780 --> 01:16:08.200
taking off before they get to school

01:16:08.200 --> 01:16:11.070
without anybody instructing
them in anything,

01:16:11.070 --> 01:16:13.860
it looks to me like it
it's associated with

01:16:13.860 --> 01:16:14.970
changes in their language.

01:16:14.970 --> 01:16:17.460
Theory of mind is another
great example of that.

01:16:17.460 --> 01:16:19.860
So then your question,
what is it about language?

01:16:19.860 --> 01:16:22.060
Now, initially, I thought that

01:16:22.060 --> 01:16:25.100
the primary feature of language

01:16:25.100 --> 01:16:29.020
that made it such a good tool
for learning about the world

01:16:29.020 --> 01:16:31.810
was that it was a compositional system.

01:16:31.810 --> 01:16:35.990
So where once you know
the meanings of words

01:16:35.990 --> 01:16:38.610
and you know the grammatical
structure of a sentence,

01:16:38.610 --> 01:16:41.350
you get the meaning of
the sentence for free.

01:16:41.350 --> 01:16:42.933
So if a child knows that a word, you know,

01:16:42.933 --> 01:16:47.090
when they hear it expression like

01:16:47.090 --> 01:16:50.250
look, Lori, a duck, okay?

01:16:50.250 --> 01:16:52.010
If they know the meaning
of the word, look,

01:16:52.010 --> 01:16:54.640
if look indicates for them

01:16:54.640 --> 01:16:58.400
an invitation to engage
and share an experience

01:17:00.110 --> 01:17:02.990
and it's directed at a specific person

01:17:02.990 --> 01:17:07.480
so they know it's for them,
and it's followed by a duck,

01:17:08.650 --> 01:17:12.230
and they know that a
duck refers to a kind,

01:17:12.230 --> 01:17:13.220
an object, you know,

01:17:13.220 --> 01:17:14.920
at least this individual object,

01:17:14.920 --> 01:17:17.410
if not yet a kind of object,

01:17:17.410 --> 01:17:20.070
that then if they hear this as a sentence

01:17:20.070 --> 01:17:23.160
expressing a specific thought,

01:17:23.160 --> 01:17:25.670
the thought has both social content

01:17:25.670 --> 01:17:28.430
and its object directed, okay?

01:17:28.430 --> 01:17:29.520
So it's both,

01:17:29.520 --> 01:17:31.270
and I think that suggests that

01:17:31.270 --> 01:17:32.630
it's reflecting a mental state

01:17:32.630 --> 01:17:35.720
that's both phenomenal and intentional,

01:17:35.720 --> 01:17:36.820
and they're getting that

01:17:36.820 --> 01:17:40.270
from the combinatorial
properties of language.

01:17:40.270 --> 01:17:42.690
But I now think actually,

01:17:42.690 --> 01:17:45.030
there's another related aspect of language

01:17:45.030 --> 01:17:48.830
that might be just as
important, which is that

01:17:48.830 --> 01:17:52.790
babies seem to already know early on that

01:17:52.790 --> 01:17:56.060
they can engage with other
people to share experiences,

01:17:56.060 --> 01:17:58.140
experiences of emotion, of action

01:17:58.140 --> 01:18:01.243
with imitation and things like that.

01:18:02.250 --> 01:18:07.250
But I think what happens at
the end of the first year

01:18:09.010 --> 01:18:11.710
is that they come to discover that

01:18:11.710 --> 01:18:15.840
they can share experiences that
cut up the world more finely

01:18:15.840 --> 01:18:18.670
than just experiences of an object.

01:18:18.670 --> 01:18:20.760
They can share experiences of an object

01:18:20.760 --> 01:18:23.170
under a particular description.

01:18:23.170 --> 01:18:26.860
So for example, when a
person says to a kid,

01:18:26.860 --> 01:18:29.620
look at that dog over there,
that's our neighbor's dog,

01:18:29.620 --> 01:18:32.680
or look at that dog, that's Fido, okay?

01:18:32.680 --> 01:18:34.210
They're using two different words.

01:18:34.210 --> 01:18:37.200
Now, if babies expect
people to be efficient,

01:18:37.200 --> 01:18:39.120
they don't expect them to
use two different words

01:18:39.120 --> 01:18:41.080
to convey the same experience.

01:18:41.080 --> 01:18:42.940
But they're talking
about the same objects,

01:18:42.940 --> 01:18:44.730
so they must be using two different words

01:18:44.730 --> 01:18:48.140
to convey two different
experiences of that object

01:18:48.140 --> 01:18:50.600
that can be shared with the child.

01:18:50.600 --> 01:18:51.720
And I think this may be

01:18:51.720 --> 01:18:54.250
really, really important for learning.

01:18:54.250 --> 01:18:57.100
Think about how you
could teach somebody that

01:18:57.100 --> 01:19:00.860
the stars up in the sky are enormous suns,

01:19:00.860 --> 01:19:02.880
you know, as large or larger than our sun

01:19:02.880 --> 01:19:04.320
and very, very far away.

01:19:04.320 --> 01:19:06.290
You could point at a star all day long

01:19:06.290 --> 01:19:08.720
and you're not gonna
be able to convey that.

01:19:08.720 --> 01:19:10.920
Language is an ideal medium

01:19:10.920 --> 01:19:13.890
for conveying those kinds of experiences.

01:19:13.890 --> 01:19:17.330
And I think that's the kind
of learning that kids get into

01:19:17.330 --> 01:19:18.840
at the end of the first year of life

01:19:18.840 --> 01:19:20.160
and throughout the preschool years.

01:19:20.160 --> 01:19:22.810
And we continue to do
it throughout our lives.

01:19:22.810 --> 01:19:24.330
But it's really central, I think,

01:19:24.330 --> 01:19:27.410
to kids learning over the
course of the preschool years.

01:19:27.410 --> 01:19:29.810
And it's just based on
fundamental assumptions

01:19:29.810 --> 01:19:32.120
that people are social,

01:19:32.120 --> 01:19:35.040
and therefore they will share
their experiences with kids,

01:19:35.040 --> 01:19:38.540
but they also are agents
who act on objects.

01:19:38.540 --> 01:19:41.240
And so they can share
experiences of objects

01:19:41.240 --> 01:19:43.050
and they will act efficiently

01:19:43.050 --> 01:19:45.610
so that when you see
what they choose to say

01:19:45.610 --> 01:19:48.160
and what they choose not to say,

01:19:48.160 --> 01:19:49.480
you can infer from that

01:19:49.480 --> 01:19:52.177
what they see as relevantly
alike in the world,

01:19:52.177 --> 01:19:53.500
and what they see is different,

01:19:53.500 --> 01:19:55.830
what distinctions they
think need to be made

01:19:55.830 --> 01:19:57.070
in the situation they're in

01:19:57.070 --> 01:19:59.890
to be sharing experiences
within that situation.

01:19:59.890 --> 01:20:01.180
Does that make any sense?

01:20:01.180 --> 01:20:04.431
Okay, those are kind of the
general ideas about language.

01:20:04.431 --> 01:20:06.860
- [Woman] Awesome, so students,
we're gonna go upstairs

01:20:06.860 --> 01:20:07.883
to (mumbles)

01:20:07.883 --> 01:20:09.653
but first, let's thank Elizabeth Spelke.

01:20:09.653 --> 01:20:10.557
- Thank you all.

01:20:10.557 --> 01:20:13.307
(all applauding)

01:20:18.502 --> 01:20:21.085
(gentle music)

